      Paper content:                                                                                                                                         
                                                                                                                                                             
      # Periodicity-aware deep learning for polymers                                                                                                         
                                                                                                                                                             
      Received: 4 January 2025                                                                                                                               
                                                                                                                                                             
      Accepted: 10 October 2025                                                                                                                              
                                                                                                                                                             
      Published online: 20 November 2025                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      # Check for updates                                                                                                                                    
                                                                                                                                                             
      Yuhui Wu1.2.5, Cong Wang12.5, Xintian Shen12, Tianyi Zhang12, Peng Zhang12.3                                                                           
      & Jian Ji@ 12.34                                                                                                                                       
                                                                                                                                                             
      Deep learning has revolutionized chemical research by accelerating the discovery and understanding of complex chemical systems. However, polymer       
      chemistry lacks a unified deep learning framework owing to the complexity of polymer structures. Existing self-supervised learning methods simplify    
      polymers into repeating units and neglect their inherent periodicity,thereby limiting the models'ability to generalize across tasks. To address this,  
      we propose a periodicity-aware deep learning framework for polymers,PerioGT.In pre-training,a chemical knowledge-driven periodicity prior is           
      constructed and incorporated into the model through contrastive learning.Then,periodicity prompts are learned in fine-tuning based on the              
      prior.Additionally,agraph augmentation strategy is employed, which integrates additional conditions via virtual nodes to model complex chemical        
      interactions.PerioGT achieves state-of-the-art performance on 16 downstream tasks.Wet-lab experiments highlight PerioGT's potential in the real        
      world,identifying two polymers with potent antimicrobial properties. Our results demonstrate that introducing the periodicity prior effectively        
      enhances model performance.                                                                                                                            
                                                                                                                                                             
      Deep learning (DL) has already had a transformative impact on chemical research owing to its powerful ability to model complex relationships within    
      chemical data.Major breakthroughs have been recently achieved in biochemistryl-3,organic chemistry4 and inorganic chemistry5.However, the development  
      of data-driven approaches in polymer science,one of the most active areas of chemistry,is stillconstrained by the field's intrinsic complexity.Polymers
      are macromolecules composed of a few to thousands of repeating units(RUs) linked by covalent bonds.This structural characteristic endows them with     
      unique physicochemical properties that are distinct from those of small molecules.In addition to the chemical structure of RUs,polymer properties are  
      modulated by various factors, including the degree of polymerization,the polydispersity index,the extent of crosslinking and the copolymer             
      architecture. The complexity of polymers provides a wide space for chemists to design polymers with various properties,yet it poses considerable       
      challenges to their modeling.                                                                                                                          
                                                                                                                                                             
      Accordingly, many efforts have been dedicated to establishing effective polymer representation for improved modeling6-9. Handcrafted features are      
      widely applied in polymer property prediction, including popular descriptors10,1l,molecular fingerprints12-15,one-hot encodings16, Polymer Genome17 and
      other task-specific features intended to complement traditional features18-21. Their performance largely depends on the quality and task-relevance of  
      the predefined features22.Sequence representations have also attracted considerable attention.In these methods,polymer chains are encoded either as    
      coarse-grained sequences23,24-which require explicit sequence information-or as line notation (such as the Simplified Molecular Input Line Entry       
      System,SMILES)of $\mathsf { R U S } ^ { 2 5 }$ ,which are then processed using language models. Other studies have explored converting line notations  
      into a binary matrix suitable for convolutional neural networks2627.Nevertheless,theserepresentationsstruggletoature thetopological complexity         
      ofpolymers28.More recently,graph-based methods represent polymers as molecular graphs oftheir RUsand show strongperformanceon some tasks29-3,but       
      theirrelianceonfixed graph construction rules limits flexibility,and deeper graph neural networks are prone to over-smoothing34.Thus,despite these     
      advances,abroadly applicable modeling approach for polymers remains elusive.                                                                           
                                                                                                                                                             
      ![](images/fcb073ed6b852b8f8ec83d1e23194aec90d3662f3c691750caee9465b2478ee2.jpg)                                                                       
      Fig.1|OverviewofPerioGT.a,The pre-training process of PerioGT.Fragments are sampled using PAand converted into PolymerGraphs,followed by the           
      application of random masking operators.Ashared graph transformer encoder extracts node-level and graph-level representations,which are used for       
      PA-based contrastive learning and masked node modeling,respectively.b,PA-based contrastive learning.Apositive fragment is sampled from the same polymer
      viaPA,while negatives are from different polymers.c,The PolymerGraph                                                                                   
      construction.Additional polymer information is incorporated via virtual nodes, which connectto allreal nodes through special edges.For                 
      copolymers,aglobal virtual node encodes sample-level features,while local virtual nodes encode component-levelfeatures.d,PGFT.Afeature extractor is    
      employed to extract representations ofthe corresponding structures in the PA.These representations are then integrated through an attention mechanism  
      and added as a prompt to the initial node features.                                                                                                    
                                                                                                                                                             
      More importantly,the structural complexity of polymers further leads to the severe scarcity of labeled data. Recently,self-supervised learning(SSL) has
      emerged asa powerfulapproach that learns informativerepresentationsfrommassveunlabeleddata35-37.Thepre-training and fine-tuning paradigm of SSL holds  
      great potential for alleviating data scarcity inchemistry.Therefore,SSLtailored for polymers offers a promising solution.In the existing SSL           
      models,polymers are simplified into RUs derived from monomers and represented as SMILES,akin to small molecules38,39.Language models are then trained  
      through masked language modeling with the processed unlabeled datasets, followedby fine-tuning for downstream tasks.However,these methods treat        
      polymers as small molecules and do not consider their periodic characteristics,which is the primary difference between polymers and small molecules.   
      Consequently,the simplification makes it difficult for the model to capture the periodic structure of polymers. This observation suggests that         
      incorporating periodicity as a prior is essential in SSL models for mitigating issues such as data scarcity and limited robustness.Several supervised  
      methods have attempted to addan edge between the head andtailofthe RU graph toapproximate the long-chain structure29.30.Nevertheless,these methods     
      still fail to adequately model the periodicity,leading to changes in polymer topology that introduce ambiguity and negatively impact model             
      generalization (see Supplementary Note1for details).Meanwhile,the BigSMILES series ofwork establishes amachine-readable line notation system that      
      precisely encodes RUs,bonding patterns and topological features,enabling structured representations across diverse polymer systems40-42 This notation  
      can be used as indexing identifiers in polymer databases ortransformed into binary vectors for modeling27.However,it encodes polymer connectivity      
      through symbolic rules,without providing an explicit mechanism for learning periodicity-aware embeddings or capturing structural invariance intrinsic  
      to polymer chains.                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
