      Moreover,the chemical structure of RUs alone is insufficient to represent polymers in certain downstream tasks.To this end, we constructed             
      PolymerGraph,which integrates additional information-such as molecular weight,crosslinking degree and copolymer type and composition-as virtual nodes  
      $\nu _ { \mathrm { a d d } }$ to improve adaptability across diverse polymer systems (Fig.lc).For copolymers,a global virtual node $\pmb { \nu } _ {   
      \mathrm { g l o b } }$ connects the subgraphs of all components,while local virtual nodes $\{ v _ { i } ^ { \mathrm { l o c a l } } \}$ are assignedto 
      their respective subgraphs. These additional conditions are not predefined butare flexibly determinedby the information available in each downstream   
      task.In addition,a virtual node with physicochemical properties was introduced to complement the graph representation (Supplementary Note 2), using    
      Mordred descriptors46 and RDKit fingerprints47for ease of computation.We suggest that modular and explicit modeling of Polymer-Graph,aligned with      
      chemicalintuition,facilitates capturing complex chemicalinteractions.                                                                                  
                                                                                                                                                             
      Prompt tuning has recently shown promise in naturallanguage processing and computer vision by modifying inputs to better utilize the knowledgelearnedin
      pre-training48-50.Inspiredbythis, we provide a periodicity PGFT strategy that aggregates the equivalent motifs in different RUs as a prompt in         
      fine-tuning. The periodicity prompt emphasizes the periodic structure of the polymer,guiding the modelto capture the environmental diversity of atoms  
      at specific locations of the polymer.Given a polymerfragment $G$ ,PA is employed to obtain a set $\{ G _ { i } \} _ { i \in [ 1 , N _ { \mathrm { a u g
      } } ] }$ containing $N _ { \mathrm { a u g } }$ RUs. For any triplet $\mathsf { a t o m } _ { i }$ bondj, $\mathsf { a t o m } _ { k }$ in G,features  
      of corresponding structures from $\{ G _ { i } \} _ { i \in [ 1 , N _ { \mathrm { a u g } } ] }$ areintegratedthroughanattentionmechanismandadded as a 
      prompt to the initial node features (Fig.ld; see ‘PolymerGraph construction',‘SSL'and 'Periodicity prompt generation'sections in Methods for full      
      details).                                                                                                                                              
                                                                                                                                                             
      # Polymer property prediction with PerioGT                                                                                                             
                                                                                                                                                             
      Polymer property prediction tasks lack benchmark datasets.To eval-uate and compare model performance, we used datasets following previous              
      studies30.39,51                                                                                                                                        
                                                                                                                                                             
      Tableland Supplementary Table1 summarize the test performance of PerioGTand baseline models on the ten regression datasets (see'Experimental           
      setup'section in Methods for details).Observations from Tablelare as follows:(1) PerioGToutperforms the baseline supervised and self-supervised models 
      onalldatasets and(2) random forests (RF)using Mordred descriptors and graph neural networks developed for small molecules generally perform worse than 
      methods specifically designedfor polymers.For example,although message passing neural networks (MPNN) and Antoniku's method (adding an edge between the
      head andtail ofan RU graph) share the samebackbone,Antoniku's method performs better on most tasks,indicating the heterogeneity of polymers and the    
      need to design methods specifically for polymers.                                                                                                      
                                                                                                                                                             
      Table 2 demonstrates the test performance of PerioGTand baselines on more complex tasks.In these tasks,each sample either includes additional          
      information along with a chemical structure or consists of a copolymer.Details regarding the specific additional conditions in each dataset are        
      provided in Supplementary Table 2.Observations fromTable2 indicate that PerioGT outperforms SSL modelsacross all datasets.Notably,PerioGT can          
      effectively model different types of copolymers,including random,alternating and block copolymers.The RF model which used the weighted sum ofthe       
      descriptorsfor each com-ponent,also performed well on the MAR-1(random copolymer) dataset.This maybe attributedto the factthat some tasks inherently   
      exhibit certain linear characteristics,and alinear addition of descriptors may also effectively capture these relationships.Overall,the results suggest
      thatPolymerGraph endows PerioGTwith a flexible mechanism to integrate additional conditions into the pre-trained model,enhancing its adaptability to   
      complex tasks involving additional conditions.                                                                                                         
                                                                                                                                                             
      As demonstrated by the above results, PerioGT achieves strong generalization across diverse downstream tasks, suggesting its potential as a robust     
      foundation for practical applications such as high-throughput polymer screening.                                                                       
                                                                                                                                                             
      # Periodicity-aware representations with PerioGT                                                                                                       
                                                                                                                                                             
      After verifying PerioGT's excellent performance on multiple down-stream tasks, we evaluated the model performance in capturing periodic features,coping
      with complex structural changes and maintaining robust representation.In the following section, we willconduct our analysis from two perspectives:     
      PA-level similarity analysis and instance-level similarity analysis.                                                                                   
                                                                                                                                                             
      PA-level similarity analysis.To assess the model's generalization to polymers not seen during pre-training, we performed PA for samples in the $T _ {  
      \mathrm { g } }$ dataset,and the average similarity between original RUs and augmentations was calculated to obtain the similarity matrix.We compared  
      PerioGTwith existing SSL models(TransPolymer and polyBERT) as wellas the commonly used Mordred descriptors.For TransPolymer and polyBERT,the publicly  
      available pre-trained weights provided by the original publications were used without any additional tuning. The similarity matrix was visualized      
      usingblock aggregation (Fig.2a). Additionally, we examined the similarity distribution between the original RUs and their augmentations across the     
      dataset (Fig.2b). It is evident that PerioGT accurately identifies different augmentations of the same polymer,with an average similarity of           
      O.84,substantially better than the baseline methods.                                                                                                   
                                                                                                                                                             
      Further, we subsequently calculated the Davies-Bouldin (DB) index of the representations above.As shown in Fig.2c,PerioGT demonstrated superior        
      clustering performance,whereas baseline methods were more sensitive to PA.On this basis,we designed a task where RUs from the same polymer were treated
      as one class and those from different polymers as distinctclasses.Amulticlass classifier was then trained using these representations,with one sample  
      per class for testing and the rest for training.To better reflect representation quality,we employed a simple $\pmb { K }$ nearest neighbors (KNN)     
      classifier. PerioGT achieved an accuracy exceeding $80 \%$ ,while the accuracy of the other two SSL methods remained below $40 \%$                     
      (Fig.2d).Additionally,we capped the number of minimal repeating units (MRUs) used in sampling at three during pre-training to balance periodicity      
      modeling and computational efficiency.Thus,we investigated the generalization ability of PerioGT with varying numbers of MRUs.As shown in Fig.2e,the   
      similarity of all types of representations decreases to varying degrees as the number ofMRUs increases.Although the number of MRUs used during         
      pre-training was capped at three, PerioGT preserves high similarity scores even when evaluated on fragments with more MRUs.                            
                                                                                                                                                             
      ![](images/4773115c310a27f0fd9c9a6c1c831957e33890dbf9274f48f08e80e5a2c2faac.jpg)                                                                       
      Fig.2|PA-level similarity analysis.a,The similarity matrices of representations generated by different approaches.Each polymer undergoes five random   
      augmentations via PA,and their representations are extracted using the pre-trained models.The similarity matrixis visualizedby block aggregation.      
      Similarity is measured using the extended Tanimoto coefficient for continuousvalued features.b,The similarity distributions estimated using KDE.The    
      dashed lines indicate the median value for each distribution.c,The clustering performance of representations learned by each method.Alower DB index    
      indicates abetter separation performance.Data are presented as mean values $\pm$ s.d. $\left( n = 3 \right)$ .d,KNN classification accuracy.In each    
      run,one sample per class was randomly selected as the test set.Data are presented as mean values $\pm \thinspace s . \mathbf { d } .$ $( n = 3 )$      
      .e,The variation of similarity with the numberof MRUs.                                                                                                 
                                                                                                                                                             
      The above results suggest that PA-based contrastive learning can effectively handle varying arrangements and lengths of polymers.This property ensures 
      that the modelcan stillrobustly identify and predict polymers even when slight variations occur in their form.                                         
                                                                                                                                                             
      Instance-level similarity analysis.To provide a more direct visualization of the model's awareness of periodicity, we presented four polymer pairs from
      the $T _ { \mathrm { g } }$ dataset and mapped their representations into two-dimensional space using $t \cdot$ distributed stochastic neighbor        
      embedding(t-SNE)(Fig.3a).                                                                                                                              
                                                                                                                                                             
      The polymers in each pair have similar structures.In the first pair, the structures of the two polyanhydrides are extremely similar: the former        
      contains two ether bonds within the alkyl chain between the two benzene rings,while the latter has only an alkyl chain between the rings,with all other
      structural features being identical.They have similar polarity and rigidity,similar mobility of chain segments and, thus,closely matched glass         
      transition temperatures ( $4 5 ^ { \circ } \mathsf { C }$ and $4 6 ^ { \circ } \mathrm { C }$ ， respectively).Despite their variability of MRU owing  
      to differences in the order of structure arrangement,PerioGTstillsuccessfully captured consistent representations of these polymers.In                 
      contrast,baseline methods encodedthem into distant positions in the latent space.In the later pairs,polymers with highly similar backbones and $T _ {  
      \mathrm { g } }$ values were also encoded as nearbyby PerioGT,but baselines produced divergent representations.The results above indicate              
      that,inaddition to identifying variants ofthe same polymer,PA-based contrastive learning forces the model to effectively learn the decisive features of
      polymers from their structure,without being disturbed by minor changes such as the size of MRUs or minor atomic substitutions.                         
                                                                                                                                                             
      To validate this statistically,PA was applied to samples in the $T _ { \mathrm { g } }$ dataset with random atom replacements.Although these modified  
      polymers do not necessarily correspond to samples in the $T _ { \mathrm { g } }$ dataset,these augmentations were analogous to thefour instance groups 
      above.We then calculated the similarity distribution between the original and the augmented RUs (Fig.3b).As expected,similarity decreased with higher  
      replacement rates;however,PerioGTconsistently maintained the highest similarity under minor perturbations,outperforming the baselines.In               
      summary,PerioGT has a better understanding of the periodicity of polymers.                                                                             
                                                                                                                                                             
      # Assessing the quality ofPerioGT representations                                                                                                      
                                                                                                                                                             
      Aftervalidating the model's understanding of periodicity,we further analyzed its representation space.A well-organized representation space should     
      filter out unnecessary details while retaining as much information as possible,which involves two aspects: alignment and uniformity52.Alignment implies
      that the model encodes similar samples into similar representations.Here we categorize it into attribute alignmentand structural alignment to          
      distinguish between the relationship ofrepresentation and polymer properties and the relationshipof representation and polymer structure.Uniformity    
      ensures representations are evenly distributed, maximizing information retention.                                                                      
                                                                                                                                                             
      Attribute alignment.We first examined whether pre-trained representations capture key physicochemical properties.Specifically, we focused on three     
      attributes including glass transition temperature, melting temperature and density.These attributes are key character-istics that reflect the behavior 
      of polymer chains,such as segmental mobility,rigidity and polarity.We calculated the mutual information between representations and these attributes   
      (Fig.4a).Additional results on structure-derived attributes are provided in Supplementary Note 3.These results indicate that PerioGT exhibits the      
      highest correlation between principal components and attributes compared with baseline methods.                                                        
                                                                                                                                                             
      ![](images/717c8fe0f80f72ebb58757e13bd4a711c9e1c577dc812ef9019d50179a109599.jpg)                                                                       
      Fig.3|Instance-level similarity analysis.a,Four groups of structurally similar polymers.Each polymer is labeled with a unique color or symboland       
      annotated with its glass transition temperature (shaded boxes).These samples were visualized using a $t$ SNEalgorithm to compare the representations   
      generated by different methods.The marked points represent the corresponding samples.                                                                  
      b,The similarity distributions of different replacement rates.Random atom replacements were applied using a predefined set offunctional groups         
      $\scriptstyle ( \mathbf { C } = \mathbf { O }$ ， N,O,CI,Br,F,S and $\scriptstyle \mathbf { C } ( = \mathbf { O } ) \mathbf { O } )$ .All modified     
      structures were sanitized using RDKit to ensure chemical validity.The dashed lines indicate the median value for each distribution.                    
                                                                                                                                                             
      To further analyze attribute alignment,a KNN algorithm was employed.Specifically, $10 \%$ of the samples from each dataset were randomly selected as   
      test data,while the remaining were used to train a KNN regressor.The trained KNN model was then evaluated on the reserved test samples.As shown in     
      Fig.4b,the KNN regressor trained on representations generated by PerioGT outperformed baseline models on most attributes,indicating that close         
      representations have similar properties.                                                                                                               
                                                                                                                                                             
      The above results demonstrate that PerioGT effectively extracts rich physicochemical knowledge of polymers after applying the proposed pre-training    
      strategy.Moreover,the strong attribute alignment in multiple tasks implies that the model possesses strong generalization capabilities,enabling it to  
      quickly adapt to unseen downstream tasks through fine-tuning.                                                                                          
                                                                                                                                                             
      Structural alignment.Structural alignment evaluates whether polymers with similar structures are mapped nearby in latent space.                        
                                                                                                                                                             
      Following prior work5o,we extracted the Murcko scaffold of each sample-defined as the core molecular framework,consisting of ring systems and          
      linkers,while excluding allsubstituent groups.Polymers with the same Murcko scaffold tend to share similar structural features. Seven major scaffolds  
      were visualized using t-SNE,and the DB index was calculated to quantify the structural alignment.As illustrated in Fig.4c,PerioGT consistently         
      exhibited a lower DB index, indicating superior clustering performance.This observation suggests that PerioGT effectively captures the core structural 
      features of MRUs, highlighting its strong capacity for structural representation and alignment in the latent space.                                    
                                                                                                                                                             
      Uniformity.Uniformity reflects whether the encoder distributes representations evenly across the latent space.We explored the uniformity using methods 
      fromprevious studies052 First,representations were generated by the pre-trained model for specific downstream tasks. Next,we reduced the dimensionality
      of these representations with t-SNE and performed L2 normalization.We visualized the normalized representation through Gaussian kernel density         
      estimation (KDE)and the probability density of radians on the unit circle.The Watson $U ^ { 2 }$ test was employed to quantitatively assess the        
      uniformity of the circular distributions,where lower values of the test statistic indicate closer agreement with uniformity.As shown in Fig.4d,PerioGT 
      produced more uniformly distributed representations in most cases,indicating its superior uniformity.                                                  
                                                                                                                                                             
      ![](images/ada7f5e8bc073d42a31720d726314acfa73c9c3ed38fe6519305d7665db65c3e.jpg)                                                                       
      Fig.4|Latent space analysis.a,Principal component analysis visualization showing mutualinformation (Ml) between principal components of different      
      representations and attributes.Points are colored with the value of the attribute. b,The performance of KNN regression using the generated             
      representations. The mean values at different kvalues on three independent runs are shown. c,t-SNE visualization of seven major Murcko scaffolds across
      different tasks.                                                                                                                                       
      Points are colored on the basis of scaffold type.Alower DB index indicates better separation performance.d, Uniformity analysis.Plots of polymer       
      representations with Gaussian KDE in $\mathbb { R } ^ { 2 }$ anda histogramof radians (that is, arctan2 $\scriptstyle ( y , x )$ for each data $( x , y
      ) \in { \mathsf { S } } ^ { 1 }$ ).TheWatson $U ^ { 2 }$ test was used to assess uniformity.Lower values of the Watson $U ^ { 2 }$ statistic indicate a
      closer fit to the uniform distribution.                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                                                             
      # Ablation study                                                                                                                                       
                                                                                                                                                             
      In the above experiments,PerioGTdemonstrated state-of-the-art performance,primarily owing to the incorporation of several advanced techniques,including
      PA-based contrastive learning,PolymerGraph (introduction of virtual nodes),masked modeling and periodicity prompts.To investigate the individual       
      contributions of these techniques to the framework,we conducted ablation studies.Consequently,we constructed six models: PerioGT-w/o VN,PerioGT-w/o    
      MNM,PerioGT-w/o CL,PerioGT-w/o PA,PerioGT-linear probe and PerioGT-prompt tuning.We evaluated the performance of these six models in ten downstream    
      tasks.To maintain consistency with previous representation learning studies and ensure fair comparisons, we froze the weights of the backbone and      
      trained aprojection head only.                                                                                                                         
                                                                                                                                                             
      The results (Supplementary Table3) indicate that each component ofPerioGTcontributes to performance in most tasks.Notably,PerioGT consistently         
      outperformed PerioGT-w/oCLand PerioGT-w/o PA in test performance,highlighting the importance ofincorporating periodicity priors into the model.        
                                                                                                                                                             
      ApplicationofPerioGT to antimicrobial polymer discovery Finally,to validate the real-world applicability of PerioGT,we conducted acase study           
      onantimicrobial polymer discovery.Antimicrobial peptides are considered a promising strategy against antimicrobial resistance but are limited by cost  
      and stability54,55. Polymers provide anattractivealternatives6-58. Unlikeantimicrobialpeptides, there is currently no large-scale dataset available for
      antimicrobial polymers.Therefore,we constructed a combinatorial polymer library using Michael addition59 and synthesized a subset for activity         
      testing(Extended Data Fig.la; see Wet-lab experimental details'section in Methods).                                                                    
                                                                                                                                                             
      PerioGTwas fine-tunedonthe labeledsetandachievedthebest predictive performance among baseline models (Supplementary Table 4). Thefine-tunedmodelwas    
      then applied to the virtuallibrary,and the top 30 predicted candidates were experimentallyvalidated.We compared the performance of PerioGT with two    
      published SSL models.With a minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC) threshold of $6 4 \mu \mathrm { g } \mathsf { m l } ^ { - 1 }$ ， PerioGT achieved a
      screening success rate of $8 3 \%$ (25 out of 30; Supplementary Table 5),much higher than baseline methods( $( 5 7 \%$ for TransPolymer and $20 \%$    
      forpolyBERT;Extended Data Fig.Ib).This result demonstrates that PerioGT exhibits superior accuracy and efficiency in identifying antimicrobial         
      polymers.                                                                                                                                              
                                                                                                                                                             
      Among the identified polymers,two leads (polymer 1and 2) exhibited potent antimicrobial activity against MRSA,with an MIC of $8 \mu \mathrm { g }      
      \mathsf { m l } ^ { - 1 }$ (Extended Data Fig.1c). MRSA cells treated with polymers 1and 2 and vancomycin hydrochloride (VM) showed strong red         
      fluorescence in live-dead staining,whereas the control group only displayed green signals (Extended Data Fig.ld).In colony counting assays,polymer     
      1reduced MRSA viability by over six orders of magnitude,while2 reduced colonies by three orders,both superior to VM (Extended Data Fig.le).We then     
      examined the antimicrobial mechanism of1and 2.Both polymers induced pronounced membrane potential disturbances in MRSAcells (Extended Data Fig.1f),and 
      transmission electron microscopy (TEM) imaging revealed severe membrane disruption with cytoplasmic leakage (Extended Data Fig.lg). These findings     
      underscore the strong interaction of polymers 1and 2 with the bacterial membrane.                                                                      
                                                                                                                                                             
      # Discussion                                                                                                                                           
                                                                                                                                                             
      Constructing DL models for polymers is of great importance but remains challenging owing to their inherent structural complexity. This work            
      demonstrates that incorporating periodicity priors-a fundamentalcharacteristic widely present in polymers-can substantially enhance the                
      generalizability and robustness oflearned representations. As an SSLframework,PerioGTcaptures essential structural semantics of polymer systems while  
      alleviating reliance on large-scale labeled datasets,making it a promising solution for data-scarce polymer science.                                   
                                                                                                                                                             
      While the PA-based contrastive strategy encourages representations of RUs from the same polymer to converge,it does not enforce complete identity.The  
      goal is to leverage periodicity priors to learn generalizable representations that improve downstream performance,rather than imposing strict          
      invariance across RUs.This contrasts with rule-based canonicalization,which standardizes structures through static rules but fails under structural    
      perturbations.By combining random masking with contrastive learning,our approach dynamically learns semantic similarity among distinct fragments,      
      yielding robust representations under variation.For tasks requiring strict invariance,a canonicalization step can be optionally applied during         
      preprocessing.                                                                                                                                         
                                                                                                                                                             
      Notably, homopolymers and alternating and block copolymers inherently exhibit periodic patterns,aligning well with the periodicity prior used in       
      pre-training.Although random copolymers lack strict periodicity,their structuralfragments may still exhibit local regularities,from which the model can
      benefit.Rather than aiming to perfectly model every polymer system,the periodicity-aware pre-training is designed to leverage this common structural   
      prior, thus offering a strong initialization.Fine-tuning on task-specific PolymerGraphs then enables adaptation to more diverse architectures.         
                                                                                                                                                             
      Although this study is limited to linear polymers owing to dataset availability,PerioGT offers afoundation for extension to more complex               
      topologies.Future investigations could incorporate topology-aware virtual nodes, including branching degree and crosslinking density, to support       
      branched,cross-linked and other architecturally diverse polymers,further expanding the applicability of the framework. Besides,one limitation of       
      PerioGTis that its internal decision process is not directly interpretable.Future efforts could explore integrating interpretable modules to help      
      elucidate the structuralfactors driving polymer properties.                                                                                            
                                                                                                                                                             
      # Methods                                                                                                                                              
                                                                                                                                                             
