      'multi-level attention', 'non-covalent interactions'], 'content': {'background': "Molecular property prediction is essential for drug development,     
      energy storage materials, and catalysis. A critical challenge is the trade-off between prediction accuracy and interpretability—most high-performing   
      models are black boxes that cannot explain their predictions. Existing interpretable GNNs like AttentiveFP and MoGAT only provide atom-level           
      importance, while Wu et al.'s work provides fragment-level attention but only evaluated on ESOL with random splits. More critically, no existing GNN   
      model handles molecules with substructures not connected by covalent bonds (salts, complexes), which are important in pharmaceutical and energy        
      applications. Furthermore, no single framework simultaneously addresses atom, bond, and fragment interpretability. Post-hoc methods like SHAP and LIME 
      struggle with graph-based molecular data because they cannot capture hierarchical relationships and specialized molecular interactions. This motivates 
      FragNet: an intrinsically interpretable GNN that reasons over four structural levels and handles non-covalent connections.", 'method': 'FragNet employs
      a hierarchical message-passing architecture operating on four graph representations: (1) Bond graph: nodes are bonds, edges connect bonds sharing a    
      common atom, initialized with bond properties (type, conjugation, ring membership, stereochemistry) and bond angles; (2) Atom graph: nodes are atoms,  
      edges are covalent bonds with features learned from the bond graph; (3) Fragment graph: created via BRICS fragmentation, nodes are fragments with      
      features aggregated from constituent atoms, edges connect adjacent fragments; (4) Fragment-connection graph: nodes represent connections between       
      fragments, enabling analysis of inter-fragment interactions. The hierarchical flow is: bond graph features → atom graph edge features → atom graph     
      updates → fragment node features → fragment graph updates. For molecules with non-covalently bonded substructures (salts, complexes), virtual          
      connections are created between fragments in different substructures to enable message passing. Each graph uses graph attention mechanisms where       
      attention weights α_ij = exp(LeakyReLU(a^T[Wh_i || Wh_j || We_ij])) / Σ_k exp(LeakyReLU(a^T[Wh_i || Wh_k || We_ik])) determine neighbor importance. The
      final molecular representation concatenates pooled atom and fragment representations. Two interpretability mechanisms are provided: (1) Attention      
      weights from each graph indicate which substructures the model focuses on; (2) Contribution values quantify fragment impact by comparing predictions   
      with/without masking: Contribution = Property^unmasked - Property^masked. Positive contributions indicate property-increasing fragments; negative      
      contributions indicate property-decreasing fragments. The model is pre-trained on self-supervised tasks before fine-tuning.', 'experiment': 'Datasets: 
      MoleculeNet benchmark with scaffold splitting—regression tasks (ESOL: 1128 compounds, LIPO: 4200 compounds, CEP: 23982 compounds) and classification   
      tasks (Clintox: 1477 compounds, Sider: 1427 compounds, Tox21: 7831 compounds). Baselines: ContextPred, AttrMask, JOAO, GraphMVP, Mole-BERT, SimSGT.    
      Training protocol: Pre-training on self-supervised tasks, hyperparameter optimization via Optuna with 3 random seeds, early stopping with patience=30, 
      scaffold splitting for realistic evaluation. For CEP, only one hyperparameter combination was tested due to computational constraints. Interpretability
      validation: (1) Case study on 1-Naphthyloxyethylethyl-beta-chloroethyl hydrochloride showing attention weights align with chemical intuition (high     
      weights on N+, Cl-, ether oxygen for solubility); (2) Aggregated analysis filtering predictions with error < 0.1 to ensure reliable interpretation; (3)
      Fragment contribution analysis across solubility, lipophilicity, and cancer drug response tasks; (4) DFT computation of electrostatic surface          
      potentials to validate contribution scores; (5) UMAP visualization showing hydrophobic/hydrophilic molecules occupy different latent space regions.',  
      'result': 'FragNet achieves SOTA on ESOL (RMSE 0.881±0.011 vs SimSGT 0.917±0.028), Clintox (AUC 86.8±1.8 vs SimSGT 85.7±1.8), and Sider (AUC 63.7±1.9  
      vs Mole-BERT 62.8±1.1), with competitive performance on LIPO (RMSE 0.682±0.031), Tox21 (AUC 76.9±0.6), and CEP (RMSE 1.092±0.031). Interpretability    
      findings align with established chemistry: (1) Heteroatoms (N, O, S) receive highest attention weights across all tasks due to their roles in hydrogen 
