      'This paper introduces polymer graph representation that explicitly captures periodicity by adding a periodic edge connecting terminal atoms of a      
      trimer, achieving 20% average error reduction across 10 polymer properties compared to hand-designed molecular descriptors and demonstrating that      
      MPNN-learned features correlate with chemically meaningful descriptors.', 'keyword': 'polymer graph, periodic representation, message-passing neural   
      network, polymer property prediction, glass transition temperature', 'content': {'background': 'Polymer informatics faces a fundamental challenge in   
      representing polymers for machine learning. Unlike small molecules with fixed atom counts, polymers consist of periodic chains with 10^0-10^6 repeating
      units, creating unique representation challenges. Existing monomer-based representations borrowed from molecular chemistry suffer from three critical  
      issues: (1) Non-unique representations - different polymers can have identical monomer representations (e.g., polymers with C-C-O repeat units but     
      different structures all map to the same monomer), leading to averaged predictions and large errors; (2) Incorrect bonding environments - functional   
      groups like ethers are artificially fragmented when forming monomer representations, corrupting fingerprinting and property descriptor calculations;   
      (3) Lack of periodicity - trivial translations along the polymer backbone produce different monomer representations for chemically equivalent          
      structures. These issues fundamentally arise from attempting to represent continuous, extended polymer chains with finite molecules, fragmenting       
      chemical bonds in unphysical ways. The authors demonstrate that even dimer representations fail to capture correct bonding environments at terminal    
      atoms. This work addresses these limitations by developing the first polymer representation to explicitly capture periodicity, drawing inspiration from
      crystal graph representations used for 3D periodic materials.', 'method': 'The polymer graph representation is constructed by taking a trimer (three   
      repeat units) and adding a periodic edge connecting the two terminal atoms, creating a closed loop that naturally encodes polymer periodicity. This    
      design ensures: (1) Unique representations for distinct polymers, (2) Correct bonding environments for all atoms including terminal positions, (3)     
      Invariance to translations along the polymer backbone. The periodic edge enables message passing throughout the entire structure, allowing atom        
      embeddings to accurately represent their true chemical environment. For example, a terminal carbon atom can now receive messages from both carbon and  
      oxygen neighbors, unlike in monomer graphs where it only sees one neighbor. The authors combine this representation with a Message-Passing Neural      
      Network (MPNN) that learns atom and bond features, aggregated into a fixed-length polymer encoding passed through a feed-forward network for property  
      prediction. The learned embeddings in the final hidden layer serve as polymer-specific descriptors optimized for the target property. Ablation studies 
      compare monomer graphs, dimer graphs, trimer graphs (without periodic edge), and polymer graphs to isolate the contribution of each design choice.',   
      'experiment': 'The dataset comprises 15,219 datapoints across 10 polymer properties: atomization energy (390), crystallization tendency (432), band gap
      chain (3,380), band gap bulk (561), electron affinity (368), ionization energy (370), refractive index (382), dielectric constant (382), glass         
      transition temperature (7,235), and density (1,719). Properties span thermal, structural, optical, and electronic types from both experimental         
      (PolyInfo database) and DFT sources. Models compared: (1) Random Forest with 200 RDKit molecular descriptors, (2) MPNN with monomer graphs, (3) MPNN   
      with polymer graphs. All models evaluated via 5-fold cross-validation with RMSE. A critical experiment tests 179 polymers where each polymer has at    
      least one other with the same monomer representation, directly quantifying the impact of non-unique representations. Additional experiments: (1) RF    
      with MPNN-learned descriptors vs RF with hand-crafted descriptors to isolate representation vs architecture effects, (2) Correlation analysis between  
      top 5 learned features and known molecular descriptors for Tg prediction.', 'result': 'The MPNN/Polymer Graph achieves 20% average error reduction vs  
      RF/Molecular Descriptors and 10% vs MPNN/Monomer Graph. Notable improvements include atomization energy (62% reduction vs RF, 33% vs monomer graph),   
      band gap chain (22% vs RF, 15% vs monomer graph), ionization energy (16% vs RF, 19% vs monomer graph), and Tg (16% vs RF, 10% vs monomer graph). On the
      critical test set of 179 polymers with shared monomers, polymer graph achieves 39°C RMSE vs 49°C for monomer graph - a 60% reduction for specific cases
      like Polymers A-C (27°C vs 62°C). Ablation reveals: periodic edge contributes 4% average improvement (32% for atomization energy), dimer vs monomer    
      provides significant gains, but trimer vs dimer shows no difference - suggesting larger graphs add computational cost without benefit. RF with learned 
      descriptors matches MPNN performance (35.34°C vs 34.91°C for Tg), demonstrating that learned representations, not architecture, drive improvements.    
      Learned features correlate with chemically meaningful descriptors: top features capture polar groups (OH, NH2) and ring structures - known determinants
      of Tg. Limitations: crystallization tendency shows no improvement (low-fidelity dataset from group contribution method); current approach focuses on   
      homopolymers.', 'github_url': 'N/A', 'dataset': 'PolyInfo database (experimental Tg and density); DFT-calculated properties (atomization energy,       
      crystallization tendency, band gap chain/bulk, electron affinity, ionization energy, refractive index, dielectric constant). Total: 15,219 datapoints, 
      9,935 unique homopolymers.', 'topic_relation': "Directly addresses Tg prediction (TopicState benchmark: Tg dataset with 7,170 samples, baseline        
      RMSE=50.5 by GAT, SOTA=30.8 by PerioGT). This paper reports MPNN/Polymer Graph achieving 34.91 K RMSE on 7,235 Tg samples. The periodic graph          
      representation aligns with the 'Data_Centric_Foundation' hypothesis about graph representation learning and the 'Advanced Algorithm Improvement'       
      direction for physics-inspired architectures. The interpretability analysis supports the MetaMethod principle of visualizing learned features.",       
      'topic_contribution': 'Provides a validated physics-inspired representation (periodic graphs) that directly encodes polymer periodicity, achieving     
      near-SOTA performance on Tg. Demonstrates that learned descriptors can match hand-crafted molecular descriptors while being interpretable. Enables     
      decision: the periodic edge design is a low-compute, high-impact architectural innovation suitable for our hardware constraints. The ablation finding  
      that dimer and trimer perform similarly suggests computational efficiency without sacrificing accuracy. The correlation analysis methodology provides a
      template for interpretability studies on our own models.', 'local_path': 'papers/day_1/PeriodicGraphs_Polymers/PeriodicGraphs_Polymers.md'},           
      'connections': {}}, {'id': 'mem_075', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'LSR-MP is a physics-informed message-passing framework that combines           
      short-range EGNN message passing with long-range bipartite atom-fragment interactions via BRICS fragmentation, achieving up to 40% MAE reduction on    
      large biomolecules while maintaining computational efficiency.', 'keyword': ['long-range interactions', 'equivariant GNN', 'fragmentation', 'molecular 
      dynamics', 'message passing'], 'content': {'background': 'Ab initio molecular dynamics (AIMD) is essential for studying chemical reactions, protein    
      folding, and electron-phonon interactions, but conventional DFT-based approaches scale prohibitively with system size (O(N^αm) with α≥3). Two main     
      approaches have emerged to address this: (1) Fragmentation-based methods from quantum chemistry that divide molecules into subsystems for parallel     
      computation, but suffer from incomplete many-body interaction modeling (typically limited to 2-3 body interactions), leading to 1-5 kcal/mol accuracy  
      limits; (2) Machine learning approaches, particularly equivariant graph neural networks (EGNNs), which achieve <1 kcal/mol accuracy on small molecules 
      but fail to capture long-range interactions (electrostatic, van der Waals) critical for large systems. EGNNs use radius cutoffs for local              
      neighborhoods, and simply increasing cutoffs or stacking layers leads to information over-squashing, data efficiency loss, and convergence             
      difficulties. The fundamental tension is that fragmentation methods capture long-range but miss many-body interactions, while EGNNs capture many-body  
      locally but miss long-range effects. This motivates a unified framework combining the strengths of both approaches.', 'method': 'LSR-MP                
      (Long-Short-Range Message-Passing) framework operates at two levels: (1) SHORT-RANGE MODULE: Standard EGNN message passing on a radius graph G_short   
      with cutoff r_short, processing atomic numbers Z and positions p to model local geometric information (bonds, angles, dihedrals). Uses iterative       
      updates: h_i^l, v_i^l = φ_short(h_i^(l-1), v_i^(l-1), Σm_ij, Σm_ij_vec). (2) FRAGMENTATION MODULE: Employs BRICS (Breaking of Retrosynthetically       
      Interesting Chemical Substructures) fragmentation using 16 predefined chemical motifs to divide molecules into chemically meaningful fragments. BRICS  
      minimizes energy discrepancies from bond breakage and applies post-processing (removing small fragments, handling duplicates, correcting overlaps).    
      Fragment representations are learned via weighted aggregation: H_j = Σα_i ⊙ h_i, V_j = Σβ_i ⊙ v_i, P_j = Σγ_i·p_i with translational equivariance      
      constraints (Σγ_i = 1, γ_i ≥ 0). (3) LONG-RANGE MODULE: Bipartite geometric message passing on G_long connecting atoms to fragments within cutoff      
      r_long >> r_short. Implements distance-dependent equivariant multi-head attention: attention weights A_ij = SiLU(sum(q_i ⊙ k_j ⊙ s_ij)) where s_ij     
      encodes size-dependent distances. Messages combine attention-weighted fragment embeddings with directional information. (4) OUTPUT: Late fusion        
      strategy concatenates short-range and long-range embeddings: h_out = DENSE([h^L_short, x^L_long]), v_out = U([v^L_short, μ^L_long]). ViSNet-LSRM       
      implements this with ViSNet backbone and includes layer normalization for fragment representations. The framework is provably equivariant and addresses
      over-squashing by rewiring negatively curved edges (bonds broken by BRICS fragmentation).', 'experiment': 'DATASETS: MD22 (large biomolecules:         
      proteins, lipids, carbohydrates, nucleic acids) and Chignolin (166-atom artificial protein with 9,543 DFT-level conformations from REMD, split 7:1:2). 
      BASELINES: sGDML, ViSNet, PaiNN, TorchMD-NET, GemNetOC, SO3krates, Allegro, MACE, Equiformer. MAIN RESULTS: On MD22, ViSNet-LSRM achieves best or      
      near-best energy/force MAE across all molecules (e.g., AT-AT-CG-CG: 0.1064 kcal/mol energy MAE vs 0.130 for Equiformer). On Chignolin: 0.667 kcal/mol  
      energy MAE vs 1.226 for Equiformer. EFFICIENCY: ViSNet-LSRM uses 1.70M parameters vs 2.21M for ViSNet, with 19s/epoch vs 44s/epoch training time.      
      ABLATION STUDIES: (1) Cutoff analysis: Increasing short-range cutoff beyond 4-5Å degrades performance due to over-squashing across all EGNNs. (2) Layer
      depth: 3-layer ViSNet-LSRM outperforms 8-layer ViSNet, showing explicit long-range modules are more effective than deepening. (3) Fragmentation        
      methods: BRICS (8.43 avg fragment size) achieves 0.1064 energy MAE vs K-means (0.1276) vs single global node (0.1482) vs no long-range (0.1563). (4)   
      General applicability: LSR-MP improves PaiNN (58.8% force MAE reduction), ET (55.0%), Equiformer (15.0%), ViSNet (24.88%) on AT-AT-CG-CG with          
      consistent 4 short-range + 2 long-range layer configuration.', 'result': 'LSR-MP successfully addresses the long-range interaction limitation of EGNNs 
      while maintaining computational efficiency O(N^α) with α=1-2. The key findings are: (1) Explicit long-range modules via atom-fragment bipartite graphs 
      are more effective than simply increasing cutoffs or depth, which cause over-squashing. (2) BRICS fragmentation provides chemically meaningful         
      fragments that outperform naive clustering (K-means) by preserving intrinsic chemical structures. (3) The framework generalizes across multiple EGNN   
      backbones (PaiNN, ET, Equiformer, ViSNet) with consistent improvements. (4) The approach is more parameter-efficient and faster than deeper baseline   
      models. LIMITATIONS: BRICS fails on supramolecules (requires fallback to canonical clustering); fragmentation is non-differentiable. FUTURE: Develop   
      differentiable fragmentation for end-to-end learning of optimal fragmentation. The work demonstrates that physics-informed inductive biases (chemical  
      locality via BRICS, bipartite graph for long-range) can effectively extend EGNNs to large molecular systems relevant for polymer science.',            
      'github_url': 'https://github.com/liyy2/LSR-MP', 'dataset': 'MD22 (large biomolecules: proteins, lipids, carbohydrates, nucleic acids), Chignolin      
      (166-atom artificial protein, 9,543 conformations from REMD)', 'topic_relation': "This paper directly addresses the 'Advanced Algorithm Improvement'   
      hypothesis in TopicState by improving molecular property prediction algorithms (specifically EGNNs) to better align with physical prior assumptions    
      (chemical locality, long-range interactions). It provides a concrete methodology for extending short-range GNN architectures to capture long-range     
      interactions, which is relevant for polymer systems where long-range interactions (electrostatic, van der Waals) are critical for properties like Tg.",
      'topic_contribution': 'LSR-MP provides an actionable framework for improving polymer property prediction models: (1) The BRICS fragmentation approach  
      can be adapted for polymer repeat units to capture both local (monomer-level) and global (chain-level) interactions. (2) The bipartite atom-fragment   
      message passing can be extended to atom-monomer or atom-chain_segment graphs for polymers. (3) The finding that explicit long-range modules outperform 
      deeper networks suggests a design principle for polymer GNNs: use shallow local processing with explicit global message passing rather than deep       
      stacking. (4) The general applicability across EGNN backbones (PaiNN, ET, Equiformer, ViSNet) provides flexibility in choosing base architectures for  
      polymer tasks. For the Tg dataset specifically, LSR-MP could capture long-range interactions between functional groups that influence chain mobility   
      and glass transition behavior.', 'local_path':                                                                                                         
      'papers/day_1/2024_LONG-SHORT-RANGE_MESSAGE-PASSING_A_PHYSICS-INFORMED_FRAMEWORK_TO_CAPTURE_NON-LOCAL_INTERACTION_/2024_LONG-SHORT-RANGE_MESSAGE-PASSIN
      G_A_PHYSICS-INFORMED_FRAMEWORK_TO_CAPTURE_NON-LOCAL_INTERACTION_.md'}, 'connections': {}}]}                                                            
