      For these experiments,we increase the system size to $1 0 0 0 0$ atoms to reduce the variance of theT estimates (see Fig. S6). This is consistent with 
      previous studies $^ { 6 1 }$ and highlights the need $^ \mathrm { g }$ for MLFFs that scale to large systems. Due to the high computational cost of    
      these simulations, we restrict this study to the 10 polymers shown in Fig. 2k-l. Furthermore,we exclude UMA from this experiment due to its slow       
      simulation speed and large memory requirements. We therefore compare Vivace only to MACE-OFF and the two classical FFs, PCFF and OPLS3e.               
                                                                                                                                                             
      The density-temperature curves generated with Vivace generally exhibit the two linear regimes characteristic of a second-order phase transition (see,  
      for instance, Fig. 4a). To our knowledge, this is the frst time an MLFF has been shown to capture second-order phase transitions in polymers. Fig. 4b  
      highlights the diffculty of this task, where the change in slope is less distinct, leading to greater prediction uncertainty. The remaining            
      density-temperature curves are provided in Fig. S7.                                                                                                    
                                                                                                                                                             
      From these curves, we extract T $_ \mathrm { g } ^ { \mathrm { \tiny { + f i t } } }$ and compare it to the experimental $\mathrm { T _ { g } }$ (Fig. 
      4c). For instance, Vivace estimates the $\mathrm { T _ { g } }$ of polystyrene to be $3 9 9 \pm 2 2 \mathrm { K }$ ，which is close to the experimental
      value of 373K. Overall, Vivace achieves an MAE of $4 3 \mathrm { K }$ across all 10 polymers, with a similar performance on seen and unseen polymers   
      (MAEs of $4 3 \mathrm { K }$ and $4 4 \mathrm { K }$ , respectively); a promising result given the task's complexity. This accuracy is comparable to   
      that of PCFF which has an MAE of $4 9 \mathrm { K }$ , with MACE-OFF and OPLS3e performing slightly worse (MAEs of $6 2 \mathrm { K }$ and 64 K,       
      respectively). Some simulations with PCFF failed due to instabilities, preventing T $^ \mathrm { g }$ determination and highlighting the limited       
      transferability of classical FFs. Further， we found that Vivace and MACE-OFF tend to overestimate T values,which is consistent with previous findings 
      made for classical FFs (see Fig. S10)61,62. Finally, we found that an accurate Tg prediction does not necessarily correlate with an accurate density   
      prediction at room temperature, and vice versa (see Figs. S8 and S9).                                                                                  
                                                                                                                                                             
