    "journal": "**Day 4 Journal: IAPT v2 Experiment - Validated Insights, Failed to Beat SOTA**\n\n**Papers Selected and Analyzed:**\nSelected 4 papers following MetaMethod criteria (2 from pool, 2 from arXiv):\n1. **Physical Pooling Functions** (arXiv 2207.13779): Pooling function selection should match property type—sum for extensive (size-dependent), mean/max for intensive. Tg is intensive.\n2. **Equivariant Benchmark** (arXiv 2008.08461): Equivariant layers provide 23% error reduction vs 4% from depth—parameter-efficient architectural innovation.\n3. **ENINet** (pool): Line graphs capture many-body equivariant interactions at triplet level, addressing vanishing directional information.\n4. **SimPoly** (pool): Polymer-specific MLFF achieving Tg MAE=43K; inter-chain interactions essential; cutoff ~6.5Å required.\n\nAll papers saved to memory (mem_086-089).\n\n**Experiment Conducted:**\nExecuted IAPT v2 (Intensive-Aware Polymer Transformer) integrating Days 1-4 insights: attention-based pooling for intensive Tg property, L1 equivariant features, line graph for angular relationships, monomer conformer features. Ran comprehensive ablation study with 6 variants.\n\n**Results:**\n- **Test RMSE: 35.08** (5-seed ensemble) vs **SOTA 30.8** (PerioGT)\n- **Gap: 4.28 RMSE points** — FAILED to beat SOTA\n- Day 1 result (34.14) remains best\n\n**Ablation Results:**\n| Variant | RMSE | Key Finding |\n|---------|------|-------------|\n| Mean pooling | 37.89 | WORST — validates intensive property insight |\n| Max pooling | 35.35 | Good |\n| Attention pooling | **35.19** | BEST single model |\n| No equivariant | 36.76 | Removing hurts ~1.1 RMSE |\n| No line graph | 36.43 | Removing hurts ~0.5 RMSE |\n\n**Validated Hypotheses:**\n1. **Intensive property insight CONFIRMED**: Mean pooling worst for Tg (intensive), attention/max best\n2. **Equivariant features help**: ~1.1 RMSE improvement\n3. **Line graph helps**: ~0.5 RMSE improvement\n\n**Critical Issues:**\n1. **Conformer generation failure**: 83% failure rate for polymer trimers (unusual atom types Cd, Pb, Zn), losing 3D geometric information\n2. **Overfitting**: Train RMSE ~12-17 vs Val RMSE ~35-37\n3. **Missing pre-training**: PerioGT's SOTA uses PI1M pretraining—we haven't used this yet\n\n**Key Insight:**\nArchitecture innovations (intensive-aware pooling, equivariant features, line graphs) are validated but insufficient. **Pre-training on PI1M is likely the critical missing piece**—PerioGT achieves 30.8 via pretraining.\n\n**Recommendations for Day 5:**\n1. Pre-training on PI1M dataset (critical missing piece)\n2. Fix conformer generation: use monomers instead of trimers\n3. Address overfitting with better regularization\n4. Consider hierarchical architecture for multi-scale polymer structure"
