      Contrastive learning with 3D network.To further extract global 3D information, we employ contrastive learning to force GNN to learn the representations
      of a3D network.The3D network encodes the pairwise Euclidean distances of all atoms and therefore completely describes the 3D structures.The pairwise   
      distances of amolecular configuration are first mapped to a higher-dimensional space using sine and cosine functions with different frequencies as edge
      features,while node features areall set to the same learnable vector that was initialized with a standard Gaussian distribution30.The features are     
      updated by multiple message-passing layers that work on fully connected graph data to generate the 3D latent code $H _ { \mathrm { b } }$              
      .Meanwhile,the same molecular configuration with perturbed coordinates are fed into a $\mathsf { G N N } f ^ { \mathrm { a } }$ to generate the GNN    
      latent code $H _ { \mathrm { a } }$ .Next,we maximize the mutual information between the output features ofGNNfaand 3D networkfb,as shown in task      
      3ofFig.1.In this way,GNNfa can learn more 3D information from 3D networkfb which can help improve downstream interatomic potential predictions.Task 3  
      can also be seen as a contrastive distillation process,where the student GNN learns from the teacher 3D network to capture 3D information.             
                                                                                                                                                             
      # Experiments                                                                                                                                          
                                                                                                                                                             
      MD17 for MD trajectories of small molecules.The MDl7 dataset5 consists of small organic molecules generated by AIMD simulations. The comparisons of the
      mean absolute error(MAE) for force predictions between GPIP-based models and baseline models are listed in Table1.The performances ofGPIP-based        
      SchNet,DimeNet,SphereNet and GemNet-Tare allbetter than their correspondingbaseline models. We also perform ablation studies to study the effect of    
      each task in GPIP and the results are summarized in Supplementary Table 3.                                                                             
                                                                                                                                                             
      ISO17 for MD trajectories of $\mathbf { c } _ { 7 } \mathbf { o } _ { 2 } \mathbf { H } _ { 1 0 }$ isomers.The ISOl7 dataset1,36 consists of MD        
      trajectories of ${ \mathbf { C } } _ { 7 } { \mathbf { O } } _ { 2 } { \mathbf { H } } _ { 1 0 }$ isomers generated by AIMD simulations.We first use a 
      small dataset of2O,OoO configurations and a large dataset of 4Oo,OoO configurations from the ISOl7 dataset for fine tuning.Two different settings are  
      considered here to evaluate the generalization ability of MLIPs across conformational space.For the unknown molecules/unknown conformations task,the   
      molecules in the test setare not in the training set.As shown in Fig.2a,b,GPIP-based models significantly outperform their non-pretrained counterparts 
      on both tasks,even thoughthefine-tuning dataset is very large.Tofurther study the data efficiency,we randomly sampled various amounts of data from the 
      original dataset for fune tuning while the test sets remain unchanged.The results of force predictions with SchNet and equivariant graph neural        
      network(EGNN) baseline models are shown in Fig.2c,d.The GPIP-based models outperform the non-pretrained counterparts throughout the experiments.We find
      that in using only 10,00O training data,the GPIP-based SchNetachieves an MAEof 9.95, muchlower than the non-pretrained SchNet withan MAE of18.O8.This  
      performance is even better than the non-pretrained SchNet trained using 1O times more training data,which results in an MAE of 10.55. Therefore,we     
      demonstrate that GPIP-based MLIPs are more data efficient than those trained from scratch in achieving comparable performance.Although the MAE         
      continues to decrease with increasing training data, considering the computational costs for acquiring high-precision labelled training data,GPIP can  
      achieve comparable accuracy with far fewer computational resources.For example,the GPIP-based SchNet achieves an MAE of 6.16 with 100,00O training     
      data,while the non-pretrained SchNet achieves an MAE of 6.81 with 200,00O training data.Using GPIP,the additional computational costs include about 23O
      central processing unit (CPU) core hours for CMD data generation and1.2 hours ofgraphics processing unit computation (using one NVIDIA AloO card) for  
      pretraining.In comparison,obtaining an additional 1oo,ooo labelled ISOl7 data by ab initio calculation requires about 53,0oo CPU core hours.           
                                                                                                                                                             
