      # Methods                                                                                                                                              
                                                                                                                                                             
      # Dataset                                                                                                                                              
                                                                                                                                                             
      We evaluate the performance of GPIP on multiple benchmark datasets ranging from simple systems,including MDl7(ref.35)and ISOl7(ref.36) to complex      
      systems with PBCs including liquid water7 and our dataset of electrolyte solutions.                                                                    
                                                                                                                                                             
      Pretraining dataset.To obtain sufficient configurations that can cover the test set,we sample a large number of configurations from CMD trajectories to
      serve as the pretraining datasets.All CMD simulations are conducted with LAMMPS software package43 and the time step is set to1fs.                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      For MD17,the pretraining datasetis composed of160,Oo0 configurations sampled from CMD trajectories performed on eight different organic molecules; for 
      ISOl7,the CMD simulations are performed on 100 isomers with atoms $\mathrm { C } _ { 7 } \mathrm { O } _ { 2 } \mathrm { H } _ { 1 0 }$ which generates
      2,0o0,0o0 configurations.These configurations are sampled with time resolution of1 ps in vacuumat different temperatures varying from 30o Kto1,Ooo K   
      with intervals of1oo Kunder canonical(NvT)ensembles,and the force field parameters were taken from OPLS-AA force field.                                
                                                                                                                                                             
      The pretraining dataset for liquid water with PBCs is generated with TIP3P force field parameters44.The trajectories contain 64 water molecules and are
      calculated at1O different temperatures from $1 0 0 \mathsf { K }$ to 1,000 K with intervals of10o K under NVT ensembles and 100,000 configurations are 
      sampled with aresolution of1 ps.The density of the configurations is determined by AIMD calculation at1atmand $3 0 0 \mathsf { K }$ with an            
      isothermal-isobaric(NPT) ensemble.                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      For the electrolyte solution pretraining dataset,we select different combinations of ions and solvents to be the initial configurations, which         
      include[Na]+ or[Li]+ cations, $\left[ \mathsf { P F } _ { 6 } \right] ^ { - }$ or $\left[ \mathsf { T f } _ { 2 } \mathsf { N } \right] ^ { - }$ anions
      and DME or 1:1EC $^ +$ DMC solvent.The concentrations of electrolyte solutions are setto be1Mor 4 M.The force field parameters were taken from the     
      OPLS-AAforce field.The CMD trajectories on l6 different electrolytes are calculated at temperatures from $1 0 0 \mathsf { K }$ to $1 { , } 0 0 0       
      \mathsf { K }$ at intervals of 100 K.Each contains 24(1M) or 48(4 M) pairs of ions and192(1M) or 72 (4 M) solvent molecules.The size of the periodic   
      box of each electrolyte solution is determinedbya5 ns simulation with NPT ensemble at 300 K,1atm,and an equilibrium stage that anneals the electrolyte 
      solution from 1,000 K to $3 0 0 \mathsf { K }$ in 10 ns was performed. Eventually, 160,00O configurations of electrolyte solution are sampled with an  
      NVT ensemble at different temperatures forlOO ns with a time resolution of1ps.                                                                         
                                                                                                                                                             
      Downstream dataset. The MD17 (ref.35),ISO17 (refs.1l,36) and liquid water37 datasets used for fine tuning are taken from previous studies.The          
      structures of electrolyte solutions are sampled from 16 CMD trajectories with different compounds and concentrations, whereas the atomic forces and    
      total energies were determined by density functional theory calculations using the Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof exchange-correlation functional45 and with   
      the projector augmented-wave pseudo-potential46.A single gamma k-point is used to sample the Brillouin zone and the cutoffenergy forthe                
      plane-wave-basis set is setto be 4OO eVand the electronic self-consistency is considered to be achieved ifthe change oftotal energy between two steps  
      is smaller than $1 0 ^ { - 5 } \mathrm { e V . }$ Each configuration contains 3(1M) or6(4 M) pairs ofions and 24 (1 M) or 9 (4 M) solvent molecules.The
      equilibrium and sampling stages of the fine-tuning dataset are identical to the pretraining dataset,and the density functional theory calculation was  
      performed on randomly selected configurations using the VASP code46 with a plane-wave basis set.There are in total 8,Ooo configurations sampled and    
      calculated.                                                                                                                                            
                                                                                                                                                             
      # Pretraining settings                                                                                                                                 
                                                                                                                                                             
      Weapply three SSLtasks (masking,denoising and contrastive learning) to learn the spatialand topologicalinformation from pretraining data. The final    
      loss function is defined as                                                                                                                            
                                                                                                                                                             
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
      { \mathcal { L } } _ { \mathrm { f i n a l } } = { \mathcal { L } } _ { \mathrm { m a s k } } + \alpha { \mathcal { L } } _ { \mathrm { d e n o i s e }
      } + \beta { \mathcal { L } } _ { \mathrm { c o n t r a s t } } ,                                                                                       
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      where $\alpha$ and $\beta$ are hyperparameters for balancing the losses of three tasks, $\mathcal { L } _ { \mathrm { m a s k } } , \mathcal { L } _ { 
      \mathrm { d e n o i s e } }$ and $\mathcal { L } _ { \mathrm { c o n t r a s t } } ,$ which are the corresponding losses for masking, denoising and    
      contrastive learning.                                                                                                                                  
                                                                                                                                                             
      In task 1,we utilize the cosine error as the criterion for reconstructing original node features.Additionally,we downweight the easy samples in the    
      training set by scaling the cosine error with a power of y where $\gamma \geq 1$                                                                       
                                                                                                                                                             
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
      \mathcal { L } _ { \mathrm { m a s k } } = \frac { 1 } { \left| \tilde { N } \right| } \sum _ { i \in \mathrm { e } } \left( 1 - \frac { { \mathbf { x 
      } _ { i } ^ { 0 } } ^ { T } \cdot \mathbf { x } _ { i } ^ { \mathrm { o u t } } } { \left\| \mathbf { \ x } _ { i } ^ { 0 } \right\| \left\| \textbf { 
      x } _ { i } ^ { \mathrm { o u t } } \right\| } \right) ^ { \gamma } , \gamma \ge 1 ,                                                                   
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      where $x _ { i } ^ { 0 }$ denotes the originalfeature, $x _ { i } ^ { \mathrm { o u t } }$ is the output ofthe decoder, $\mathcal { C }$ is the set of 
      indices ofthe masked nodes $\tilde { N }$ and the scaling factor y is a hyperparameter that can be adjusted according to different datasets.           
                                                                                                                                                             
      In task 2,the GNN modelis trained to predict the additional noise under the challenge of randomly masked atoms,and the objective function is           
                                                                                                                                                             
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
      \mathcal { L } _ { \mathrm { d e n o i s e } } = \mathbb { E } _ { p ( \hat { R } , \tilde { X } ) } \left[ \left\| f _ { \theta } ( \tilde { X } ,    
      \hat { R } ) - \varepsilon \right\| _ { 2 } ^ { 2 } \right] ,                                                                                          
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      where $f _ { \theta }$ denotes an invariant/equivariant GNN model parameterized by $\theta$ and $p ( \hat { R } , \tilde { X } )$ measures the         
      probability distribution of perturbed molecular conformations.                                                                                         
                                                                                                                                                             
      In task 3,the GNN is forced to learn the 3D information from 3D network by maximizing the mutual information between the latent features ofthese two   
      nets.The corresponding loss function is:                                                                                                               
                                                                                                                                                             
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
      \mathcal { L } _ { \mathrm { c o n t r a s t } } = - \frac { 1 } { n } \sum _ { i = 1 } ^ { n } \left[ \log \frac { \mathrm { e } ^ { \mathrm { s i m }
      \left( \mathbf { h } _ { i } ^ { a } , \mathbf { h } _ { i } ^ { \mathrm { b } } \right) / \tau } } { \sum _ { k = 1 } ^ { n } \mathrm { e } ^ {       
      \mathrm { s i m } \left( \mathbf { h } _ { i } ^ { a } , \mathbf { h } _ { k } ^ { \mathrm { b } } \right) / \tau } } \right] ,                        
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      where $\sin \left( \mathbf { h } ^ { \mathrm { a } } , \mathbf { h } ^ { \mathrm { b } } \right) = \mathbf { h } ^ { \mathrm { a } } \cdot \mathbf { h 
      } ^ { \mathrm { b } } / \left( \left\| \mathbf { h } ^ { \mathrm { a } } \right\| \left\| \mathbf { h } ^ { \mathrm { b } } \right\| \right)$ is the   
      cosine similarity, $\mathbf { h } ^ { \mathrm { a } }$ and $\boldsymbol { \mathsf { h } } ^ { \flat }$ are latent codes ofGNN and 3D                   
      network,respectively,and $\tau$ is ahyper-parameter that can be seen as the weight for the most similar negative pair.                                 
                                                                                                                                                             
      The pretraining settings are determined according to the dataset during the experiments.Forthe MDl7dataset,allinvariant or equivariant GNN modelsare   
      trained for three epochs with an initiallearning rate of 0.00land a weight decay of O.Ol during pretraining.We set $\alpha = 1$ and $\beta = 1$ in     
      equation(3).The models are first pretrained on the CMD data of all molecules and then fine-tined on the labelled data of each molecule separately.We   
      utilize the Adam optimizerwith a batch size of1Oo.For the ISOl7 dataset,we train the GNN models for three epochs with GPIP. The mask rate and batch    
      size are set to O.15 and 1Oo,respectively. We set $\alpha = 1$ and $\beta { = } 0 . 5$ in equation (3).The Adam optimizer is utilized with a learning  
      rate of $5 \times 1 0 ^ { - 5 }$ and a weight decay of 0.01.                                                                                           
                                                                                                                                                             
      For the two datasets with PBCs,we train the GNN models for three epochs with GPIP.We employed the Adam optimizer with an initial learning rate of $4   
      \times 1 0 ^ { - 3 }$ and a weight decay of O.O1. Additionally, we set the pretraining loss with $\alpha = 1$ and $\beta { = } 0 . 1$ in equation (3). 
      We use a mask rate of O.15 and a batch size of1Oo for the liquid water dataset and a mask rate of O.O5 and a batch size of three for the electrolyte   
      solution dataset.                                                                                                                                      
                                                                                                                                                             
      # Fine-tuning settings                                                                                                                                 
                                                                                                                                                             
      All experiments are implemented by PyTorch v.1.8.0.We construct theloss function to fit the totalenergy Eand theforce $\mathbf { F } _ { i }$ on each  
      atom:                                                                                                                                                  
                                                                                                                                                             
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
      \mathcal { L } _ { \mathrm { f i n e - t u n e } } = | | E - \hat { E } | | ^ { 2 } + \frac { 1 0 0 } { n } \sum _ { i = 1 } ^ { n } \left| \left|     
      \mathbf { F } _ { i } - \left( - \frac { \partial \hat { E } } { \partial \mathbf { r } _ { i } } \right) \right| \right| ^ { 2 } ,                    
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      where $n$ is the number of atoms in a data sample, $\mathbf { r } _ { i }$ represents the coordinates of atom iand $\hat { E }$ denotes the            
      predictedenergy.The training setup varies when GNNs are trained on different datasets and we show the details as follows.                              
                                                                                                                                                             
      MD17.The models are trained for 5OO epochs.Both training and validation sets consist of1,Ooo configurations.The parameters of the pretrained embedding 
      layersand message-passing layers are transferred during the fine-tuning process.All hyperparameters for each GNN are based on the recommendations from 
