      graph, domain knowledge, bioisostere, pretraining, content: {'background': "Graph neural networks (GNNs) have achieved state-of-the-art performance on 
      graph-related tasks including drug-related problems in the biomedical domain. However, like most deep learning architectures, GNNs require large       
      amounts of labeled data, while task-specific labels in real-world biomedical applications (e.g., drug responses from biological experiments) are       
      expensive and time-consuming to obtain. Self-supervised pretraining on GNNs has been actively explored, with contrastive learning emerging as a        
      promising approach by maximizing mutual information between paired graph augmentations. However, current graph contrastive learning frameworks face two
      critical limitations: (1) Augmentations designed for general graphs (node dropping, edge perturbation, subgraph extraction, attribute masking) may not 
      be suitable for molecular graphs—random perturbations can alter molecular semantics (e.g., dropping a carbon atom in aspirin's phenyl ring breaks the  
      aromatic system, resulting in an alkene chain). (2) The contrastive scheme only learns representations invariant to local perturbations, neglecting the
      global structure of the dataset where some graphs should be closer in embedding space due to structural similarity. This work addresses these          
      limitations by infusing domain knowledge at both local and global levels specifically for molecular graphs.", 'method': 'MoCL proposes a double        
      contrast objective integrating local-level and global-level domain knowledge:\n\n**Local-level Domain Knowledge (Substructure Substitution)**:\nUnlike 
      general augmentations that randomly perturb nodes/edges, MoCL introduces bioisostere-based augmentation where valid substructures are replaced by      
      bioisosteres—molecular fragments with similar physical/chemical properties. The authors compiled 218 substitution rules from domain resources plus 12  
      additional rules for general carbon groups (230 total). Rules are represented as SMARTS strings (e.g., carboxylic acid → nitrogen heterocycle). The 36 
      unique source substructures are categorized into 8 groups: CA (carboxylic acid), Ester, Ketone, Phenyl, Tbutyl, dsAmide, msAmide, nsAmide. Key         
      advantage: variation is introduced without altering molecular properties. Multiple substitutions can be applied for diversity, with rule availability  
      updated after each round.\n\n**Global-level Domain Knowledge**:\nMolecular similarity is calculated using Extended Connectivity Fingerprints (ECFPs)   
      with Tanimoto coefficient: s(e₁, e₂) = N₁₂/(N₁ + N₂ - N₁₂). Two strategies are proposed:\n1. Direct supervision: Least square loss between predicted   
      and target similarity\n2. Contrastive objective: Similar graph pairs have higher mutual information vs background\n\n**Overall Framework**:\n1.        
      Generate two augmented views G¹, G² from original graph G using substructure substitution\n2. Feed G¹, G², G through GNN encoder f → representations   
      h¹, h², h\n3. Apply projection head g → embeddings z¹, z², z\n4. Optimize: L = L^local + λL^global\n\n**Theoretical Justification**:\nLemma 1 shows the
      objective is equivalent to triplet loss in metric learning, pulling positive pairs close while pushing negative pairs apart from both local and global 
      perspectives.', 'experiment': '**Datasets**: 7 benchmark molecular datasets covering diverse tasks:\n- bace (1513 molecules, binding results with human
      proteins)\n- bbbp (2050 molecules, blood-brain barrier penetration)\n- clintox (1483 molecules, FDA clinical trial toxicity)\n- mutag (188 molecules,  
      mutagenic effects)\n- sider (1427 molecules, adverse drug reactions)\n- tox21 (7831 molecules, in vitro toxicity screening)\n- toxcast (8597 molecules,
      617 tasks, toxicity assays)\n\n**Evaluation Protocols**:\n1. Linear protocol: Fix pretrained representations, train linear classifier\n2.              
      Semi-supervised protocol: Sample ~100 molecules for training, finetune all layers\n\n**Model Architecture**: GIN (3 layers, 512-dim hidden, 128-dim    
      embedding) with edge features. Projection head: 2-layer MLP. Cosine similarity for contrastive loss.\n\n**Baselines**:\n- Vanilla GNN (scratch         
      training)\n- Self-supervised: InfoGraph, EdgePred, ContextPred, Masking\n- Graph contrastive learning: node dropping, edge perturbation, subgraph      
      extraction, attribute masking\n- MICRO-Graph (motif-based contrastive)\n\n**Implementation**: Pretrain 100 epochs, batch size 32, Adam optimizer       
      (lr=0.001), dropout 0.5. Augmentation ratio 0.2 for general augmentations, augmentation times {1,2,3,5} for MoCL-DK. λ ∈ {0.5, 1.0, 5.0, 10.0} for     
      global loss. ECFP dimension 1024 for similarity calculation.', 'result': '**Main Results**:\nMoCL-DK (local knowledge only) outperforms all general    
      augmentations and self-supervised baselines on most datasets. MoCL-DK-G (with global knowledge) further improves performance. Best results achieved by 
      combining MoCL-DK with attribute masking.\n\n**Linear Protocol (AUC)**:\n- MoCL-DK: bace 0.801, bbbp 0.870, clintox 0.727, mutag 0.950, sider 0.615,   
      tox21 0.740, toxcast 0.636\n- MoCL+AttrMask: bace 0.831, bbbp 0.892, clintox 0.695, mutag 0.947, sider 0.623, tox21 0.768, toxcast 0.653\n-            
      MoCL-DK-G(LS): bace 0.831, bbbp 0.892, clintox 0.724, mutag 0.958, sider 0.623, tox21 0.777, toxcast 0.659\n\n**Semi-supervised Protocol**: Consistent 
      improvements over baselines, demonstrating effectiveness when labels are scarce.\n\n**Ablation Studies**:\n1. Augmentation combinations: MoCL-DK +     
      attribute masking performs best, validating complementary nature of semantics-preserving and attribute-based augmentations.\n2. Global loss strategies:
      Contrastive objective outperforms direct supervision (least square loss).\n3. Hyperparameters: λ=1.0, neighbor size ~10 work well; performance degrades
      with too large λ or neighbor size.\n\n**Key Insights**:\n1. Domain-aware augmentation preserves molecular semantics better than random perturbation\n2.
      Global similarity knowledge enriches representations with dataset structure\n3. Bioisostere rules are chemically meaningful and transferable\n4.       
      Theoretical connection to triplet loss explains effectiveness\n\n**Limitations**:\n1. Substitution rules require domain expertise to compile\n2. Rules 
