DEBUG {'status': 'success', 'count': 1, 'memories': [{'id': 'mem_076', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'PerioGT introduces a periodicity-aware deep learning
      framework for polymers that incorporates chemical knowledge-driven periodicity priors through contrastive learning and prompt-guided fine-tuning,      
      achieving state-of-the-art performance on 16 downstream tasks and successfully identifying two antimicrobial polymers with potent activity against     
      MRSA.', 'keyword': 'polymer deep learning, periodicity-aware, contrastive learning, graph transformer, self-supervised learning', 'content':           
      {'background': 'Deep learning has transformed chemical research across biochemistry, organic chemistry, and inorganic chemistry, yet polymer science   
      lacks a unified framework due to intrinsic structural complexity. Polymers are macromolecules composed of repeating units (RUs) linked by covalent     
      bonds, with properties modulated by degree of polymerization, polydispersity index, crosslinking extent, and copolymer architecture. Existing          
      representation methods include handcrafted features (molecular fingerprints, Polymer Genome), sequence representations (SMILES with language models),  
      and graph-based methods, but all have limitations: handcrafted features depend on feature quality, sequence representations struggle with topological  
      complexity, and graph neural networks suffer from over-smoothing. Critically, existing self-supervised learning methods simplify polymers into RUs     
      treated as small molecules, neglecting their inherent periodicity—the primary difference between polymers and small molecules. This simplification     
      limits model generalization across tasks. Supervised methods adding edges between RU head and tail fail to adequately model periodicity, introducing   
      topological ambiguity. The severe scarcity of labeled polymer data further motivates SSL approaches. An ideal framework must explicitly model both     
      intrinsic periodicity and behavior under various conditions.', 'method': "PerioGT proposes four key technical innovations: (1) Periodicity Augmentation
      (PA): A chemical knowledge-driven sampling module serving as periodicity prior. PA samples fragments from the same polymer with different arrangement  
      orders and degrees of polymerization (up to 3 MRUs during pre-training), providing multiple equivalent views of the same polymer. (2) PA-based         
      Contrastive Learning: Fragments from the same polymer via PA serve as positives, while fragments from different polymers are negatives. A shared graph 
      transformer encoder extracts node-level and graph-level representations. Contrastive learning aligns semantically similar fragments, enhancing global  
      structural understanding beyond masked language modeling's local dependencies. (3) PolymerGraph Construction: A modular graph augmentation strategy    
      integrating additional information (molecular weight, crosslinking degree, copolymer type/composition) as virtual nodes. The SMILES of RU is converted 
      to molecular graph, then transformed to line graph where nodes represent bonds and edges represent bond adjacency. For copolymers, a global virtual    
      node connects all component subgraphs, while local virtual nodes encode component-level features. Virtual nodes with physicochemical properties        
      (Mordred descriptors, RDKit fingerprints) complement graph representation. (4) Periodicity Prompt-Guided Fine-Tuning (PGFT): Aggregates equivalent     
      motifs from PA-augmented fragments through attention mechanism, added as prompt to initial node features. This guides the model to capture             
      environmental diversity of atoms at specific polymer locations. The architecture uses line graphs (nodes = bonds, edges = bond adjacency) with graph   
      transformer encoder, showing strong performance on chemistry tasks. Pre-training combines PA-based contrastive learning at graph level with masked node
      modeling at node level.", 'experiment': "The evaluation uses 16 downstream tasks: 10 regression datasets (Tg: 7,170 polymers, Tm: 3,651, Egc: 3,380, P:
      1,693, Egb: 561, Eat: 390, ε: 382, E: 370, Eea: 368, nc: 382) following previous studies, and 6 tasks with additional information (EA: 42,966, IP:     
      42,966, OPV: 1,203, MAR-1: 520, MAR-2: 520, PE: 271) including block/alternating/random copolymers. Baselines include RF with Mordred descriptors,     
      MPNN, GAT, Graphormer, LiGhT, GraphGPS, Antoniku's method (head-tail edge), Aldeghi's method, polyBERT, and TransPolymer. For representation quality   
      analysis, pre-trained weights from TransPolymer and polyBERT were used without tuning. PA-level similarity analysis computed average similarity between
