      SI Supporting Information                                                                                                                              
                                                                                                                                                             
      ABSTRACT: Neural network models excel in molecular property predictions but often struggle with generalizing from smaller to larger molecules due to   
      increased structural diversity and complex interactions. To address this, we introduce an E(3) invariant (and equivariant capable） message passing    
      graph neural network (GNN), namely, X2-GNN, that integrates physical insights via atomic orbital overlap integrals and core Hamiltonians. These        
      features provide essential information about bond strength, electron delocalization, and many-body interactions, enhanced by an atention mechanism for 
      improved learning efficiency. Benchmarked against mainstream GNNs on diverse data sets, X2-GNN trained solely on the QM9 data set (up to nine heavy    
      atoms) effectively generalizes to larger molecules with tens of heavy atoms,achieving credible per-atom error rates. It also excels in potential energy
      surface modeling and accurately predicts the bond dissociation energy within subseconds. These results highlight X2-GNN's scalability and broad        
      applicability, emphasizing the importance of integrating data-driven approaches with basic knowledge from electronic structure theory.                 
                                                                                                                                                             
      ![](images/53ccc97dd91aeb3f6042f3d8cf541a3bf19dfab67b47b0320a1dc991e4372006.jpg)                                                                       
                                                                                                                                                             
      Ds effort,often involving a trial-and-error process based on experiments. Large-scale computational exploration of uncharted chemical space is poised  
      to transform this approach into a more data-driven process.1-4 However，a trade-off between computational speed and predictive accuracy exists in the  
      context of computational chemistry.While first-principlesbased methods,such as density functional theory (DFT),can provide more accurate and broadly   
      applicable descriptions, their relatively high computational cost limits their use in largescale systems.On the contrary，classical force felds and    
      semiempirical methods offer more computationally efficient options, but they may suffer from lower accuracy when applied to systems that fall outside  
      their parametrization range and hence are less transferable.                                                                                           
                                                                                                                                                             
      A neural network (NN)，as a universal function approximator，holds great potential to overcome this trade-off between computational speed and          
      predictive accuracy. Benefiting from advancements in hardware and algorithms, NN models for chemical systems can now encompass millions or more        
      parameters,be trained on vast data sets,and be applied to large-scale systems very inexpensively.58 However, NN models_are well recognized as being    
      effective for interpolation, meaning they excel at handling cases within the distribution of those encountered during training. Meanwhile，the size of 
      the chemical compound space expands exponentially with an increase in the number of element types, constituent atoms,andbonding                        
      configurations.10Foristanc,                                                                                                                            
                                                                                                                                                             
      GDB-17,11 asynthetically generated data setof organic, druglike molecules containing up to 17 non-hydrogen atoms (H, C, N,O，S，and halogens)，includes
      ${ > } 1 6 6$ billion molecules. Despite its enormity， this data set represents only a tiny fraction of the possible structures that could be formed  
      under basic bonding rules for a given formula. Moreover, the true scope of chemical space is far beyond the so-called chemical compound space as each  
      molecule with a given constituent structure always has a number of conformations.This amount can also increase rapidly,depending on factors such as the
      number of rotatable bonds and chiral centers.The inclusion of metastable or even non-equilibrium states further complicates the landscape,potentially  
      making it infinitely complex.                                                                                                                          
                                                                                                                                                             
      The computational cost of applying high-precision frstprinciples methods to large-scale systems also increases exponentially with system size，making  
      it increasingly impractical to continuously expand the data sets to boost the predictive power of NN models. Consequently, training NN models using    
      manageable data sets while ensuring their applicability to more diverse and larger-scale systems is a significant challenge,aiming to surpass the      
      limitations of firstprinciples methods. Therefore， developing physically meaningful NN models capable of transcending the limitations of merely       
      interpolating between known data points is a key to future advancements.                                                                               
                                                                                                                                                             
      ![](images/c7c64f389f7d9f65b682835e64b5dc7ff79ad2d7df951db297385179606fe572.jpg)                                                                       
      Figure1.ArcetureofG(a)Oveallcitectuof.(b)etaldrcturesoftembdgblckdeeacbok in X2-GNN,as well as the message passing layer and the residual block in the 
      interaction block.                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      A particular class of NN models, i.e., graph neural network (GNN)，has demonstrated remarkable accuracy and efficiency.56,1216GNsrepresent             
      moleculesasfeatured e metric graphs and extract structural representations in an endto-end manner. Basically, the performance improvement of GNNs can  
      be achieved by either more complete structural representations $1 2 - 1 4 , 1 7 - 2 0$ or more powerful model architectures.5,6,21-23 Building on these
      two aspects，we develop a generally applicable and physically featurized end-to-end machine learning (ML） method，using some symmetryadapted          
      electronic structure integrals, incorporating both atomic orbital overlap integrals (S）and core Hamiltonian integrals $\left( H ^ { \mathrm { c o r e 
      } } \right)$ . We designate this method as X2-GNN, representing the next generation of the X1 methods.2 24 -29 By integrating these electronic         
      structure integral features with a message passing algorithm enhanced by an attention mechanism,031 X2-GNN learns robust molecular representations     
      suitable for a wide array of tasks on molecular property prediction.Notably, it exhibits a high level of generalization to out-of-distribution data,   
      even when trained only on some druglike small molecules.                                                                                               
                                                                                                                                                             
      In most GNNs, the basic molecular representation is atom embedding. During training， the embedding vectorsare optimized,and inductive bias from the   
      training set is introduced.Quantum chemical (QM） methods can help in setting up unbiased representations,in which molecules are systematically        
      described by QM matrices such as the overlap matrix and density matrix.Previous works have demonstrated the feasibility of incorporating these matrices
      into GNNs.14,15 For instance,certain semiempirical methods,such as GFN-$\mathbf { x T B } , ^ { 3 2 }$ havebeenused in OrbNet4andOrbNet-Equi15 which   
      avoid significant computational overhead if full DFT calculations are utilized. Full DFT calculations require solving the Kohn-Sham equations          
      iteratively and involve multiple steps of computationally intensive operations. On the contrary, the calculations of the S and $H ^ { \mathrm { c o r e
      } }$ integrals are signifcantly more straightforward and computationally efficient,even for large systems and with large basis sets.These quantities   
      capture fundamental aspects of the electronic structure of molecules and can serve as valuable inputs for ML models, particularly in the context of    
      GNNs. Considering this， we propose here to use these basic QM integrals, i.e., S and $H ^ { \mathrm { c o r e } }$ to construct molecular             
      representations suitable for GNNs:                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
      \begin{array} { l } { S _ { \mu \nu } = \displaystyle \int \phi _ { \mu } \phi _ { \nu } ^ { * } \mathrm { d } \tau } \\ { \displaystyle H _ { \mu \nu 
      } ^ { \mathrm { c o r e } } = \int \phi _ { \mu } h _ { \mathrm { c o r e } } \phi _ { \nu } ^ { * } \mathrm { d } \tau } \end{array}                  
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      where $\phi _ { \mu }$ and $\phi _ { \nu }$ are atomic orbitals indexed by $\mu$ and $\nu _ { . }$ ， respectively, $\begin{array} { r } { h _ {       
      \mathrm { c o r e } } = - \frac { 1 } { 2 } \nabla ^ { 2 } + \sum _ { A } \frac { Z _ { A } } { | r - R _ { A } | } } \end{array}$ is the core         
      Hamiltonian operator, $r$ is the coordinate of the electron, and $R _ { A }$ is the coordinate of nucleus indexed by $A$                               
                                                                                                                                                             
      Because S and $H ^ { \mathrm { c o r e } }$ are high-order equivariants in the context of group theory， their matrix element changes with respect to  
      molecular rotations. Therefore, it is necessary to symmetrize them before they are put into a rotationally invariant NN. This is achieved by           
      calculating the $L _ { 2 }$ norm of elements belonging to the same atomic orbital shell. The procedure can be formulated as follows:                   
                                                                                                                                                             
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
      S _ { l _ { 1 } , l _ { 2 } } ^ { s y m } = \sqrt { \sum _ { \phi _ { \mu } \in l _ { 1 } } \sum _ { \phi _ { \nu } \in l _ { 2 } } { S _ { \mu \nu } }
      ^ { 2 } }                                                                                                                                              
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
      H _ { l _ { 1 } , l _ { 2 } } ^ { \mathrm { c o r e , s y m } } = \sqrt { \sum _ { \phi _ { \mu } \in l _ { 1 } } \sum _ { \phi _ { \nu } \in l _ { 2 }
      } H _ { \mu \nu } ^ { \mathrm { c o r e 2 } } }                                                                                                        
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      where $l _ { 1 }$ and $l _ { 2 }$ in eqs 3 and 4 represent atomic orbital subshells. $\mathbf { S } ^ { \mathrm { { s y m } } }$ and $\mathbf { H } ^ {
      \mathrm { c o r e } , \mathrm { s y m } }$ are the symmetry-adapted overlap matrix and the core Hamiltonian matrix, respectively. Given an $N \times N$
      integral matrix, this adaption results in an $M \times M$ rotationally invariant matrix, where $N$ is the number of atomic orbitals and $M$ is the     
      number of subshels. The symmetryadapted matrix obtained from ${ \bf H } ^ { \mathrm { c o r e } }$ is additionally normalized by the number of         
      electrons to keep the same numerical magnitude as $\mathbf { S } ^ { \mathrm { { s y m } } }$ .After this normalization，the blocks corresponding to   
      atom pairs in the symmetrized matrix are flattened into feature vectors.Features from $\mathbf { S } ^ { \mathrm { { s y m } } }$ and $\mathbf { H } ^ 
      { \mathrm { c o r e } , \mathrm { s y m } }$ are concatenated as atom pair features in the molecular graph. A survey of the time cost for this feature 
      preparation process is provided in Table S1 and Figure S1 in section S1 of the Supporting Information.                                                 
                                                                                                                                                             
      The choice of different basis sets naturally provides an adjustment in efficiency and accuracy. Larger basissets describe atomic orbitals more         
      accurately. Therefore, the S and $H ^ { \mathrm { c o r e } }$ integral features obtained can better capture the atomic environment. In this work, we  
      primarily utilized the 6- 311+G(3df,2p) basis set33,34                                                                                                 
                                                                                                                                                             
      ![](images/0d6a92abe7efaac80febde1cf23f85fe4ae37566c8e378c07de47e3060e66c3c.jpg)                                                                       
      Figure2.TrasforatioofolculefteodinateepresentatiotdgegapresetatioTleculeepreseedbytoc coordinatesdcbrs(a),sfsttafodcedeah)chisrdt which directed atom  
