      Compared to other methods that exploit QM matrices as NN inputs, such as OrbNet,14 NeuralXC,4 and OrbNet-Equi,15 X2-GNN does not involve calculations  
      of any two-electron integral and does not require a complete QM calculation to provide intermediate matrices (Fock matrix or density matrix, for       
      example) or energy bases.Unlike OrbNet,14 which achieves rotational invariance through a symmetry adaption of atomic orbitals, X2-GNN performs its     
      symmetry adaption operation directly on the integral results.                                                                                          
                                                                                                                                                             
      Because the features represent atom pair relationships, X2- GNN follows the idea of directional message passing proposed by Gasteiger et al.13,35The   
      model uses edge graphsto represent the molecules.The overall architecture of X2-GNN is shown in Figure 1. The transformation from inputs, i.e., the    
      atomic positions and atomic numbers (Figure 2a),into a graph representation is performed by an embedding block. The embedding block first builds a     
      bidirectional atom graph with atoms as nodes and directed atom pairs as edges (Figure 2b), according to cutoff value c. Then, the atom node graph is   
      used to build an edge graph (Figure 2c),in which directed atom pairs are nodes and directed atom triplets are edges.                                   
                                                                                                                                                             
      The edge graph data are fed into four successive interactionreadout complex blocks.Each interaction block consists of a message passing layer,a layer  
      normalization layer, and several residualblocks.To fully utilize the information in the S and $H ^ { \mathrm { c o r e } }$ integral features，a graph 
      attntion mechanism31,38 is incorporated into the message passing layer. The attention coefficient is calculated using the representations of           
      neighboring atom pairs and the atom between them:                                                                                                      
                                                                                                                                                             
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
      \alpha _ { k j , j i } = \mathrm { s o f t m a x } \left[ \frac { ( x _ { j i } \mathbf { W } _ { K } ) ^ { \mathrm { T } } ( x _ { k j } \mathbf { W }
      _ { Q } + e _ { j } \mathbf { W } _ { Q _ { e } } ) } { \sqrt { d } } \right]                                                                          
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      where $\alpha _ { k j , j i }$ is the attention coefficient from the directed atom pair $j i$ to $k j$ ， ${ \bf W } _ { K } \thinspace { \bf W } _ { Q
      } ,$ and ${ \bf W } _ { Q _ { e } }$ are learnable weight matrices, and $d$ is the column dimension of these weight matrices. $x _ { j i }$ and        
      $\boldsymbol { x } _ { k j }$ are the representations of directed atom pairs $j i$ and $k j$ ， respectively. $e _ { j }$ is the representation of atom
      j.                                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      As demonstrated in Figure lb, the geometric information in the message from the source atom pair is extracted by a radial basis function (RBF）filter  
      and an angular filter using the spherical Bessel function (SBF).This information is then aggregated to the target atom pair using the attention        
      coefficient.In the readout blocks,all atom pair features are aggregated into atom features and a multilayer perceptron (MLP） maps these atom          
      representations to atom energies. Summation of all readout block outputs gives predicted atom energy $E _ { i }$ .The total energy of the molecule is  
      the summation of all atom energies:                                                                                                                    
                                                                                                                                                             
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
      E = \sum _ { i } E _ { i }                                                                                                                             
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      where i represents the indices of the atoms.                                                                                                           
                                                                                                                                                             
      To satisfy the continuity requirement in modeling of the potential energy surface (PES), all inputs related to pairwise distances in X2-GNN (including 
      the S and $H ^ { \mathrm { c o r e } }$ integral features，radial expansions,and angular expansions）are smoothed using an envelope function. We use   
      the envelope function employed in DimeNet:13                                                                                                           
                                                                                                                                                             
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
      u ( d ) = 1 - { \frac { ( p + 1 ) ( p + 2 ) } { 2 } } d ^ { p } + p ( p + 2 ) d ^ { p + 1 } - { \frac { p ( p + 1 ) } { 2 } } d ^ { p + 2 }            
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      where $u ( d )$ refers to the envelope function, $p$ is a hyper-parameter，and $d$ is the pairwise distance.By ensuring continuity，one can obtain a   
      conservative force prediction by directly differentiating the energy prediction with respect to the atomic coordinates:                                
                                                                                                                                                             
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
      \overrightarrow { F _ { a } } = - \nabla _ { a } E _ { \mathrm { t o t a l } }                                                                         
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      where $^ a$ represents an atom in the molecule.For X2-GNN, the contribution of the integrals to the gradient must be considered:                       
                                                                                                                                                             
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
      \overrightarrow { F _ { a _ { k } } } = \sum _ { \mu \nu } \frac { \partial f } { \partial I _ { \mu \nu } } \frac { \partial I _ { \mu \nu } } {      
      \partial R _ { a _ { k } } } + \frac { \partial f } { \partial R _ { a _ { k } } } , k \in \left( x , y , z \right)                                    
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      where $f$ represents the NN model, $I$ represents the integral feature, $\mu$ and $\nu$ are the indices of atomic orbitals,and $R _ { a }$ is the      
      coordinate of atom indexed by $^ a$ .The gradients of the S and ${ \bf H } ^ { \mathrm { c o r e } }$ matrices with respect to atomic coordinates can  
      be conveniently computed using QM programs such as PySCF.39 More implementation details may be found in section S2.                                    
                                                                                                                                                             
      First,X2-GNN is tested on the task of molecular property prediction using the $\boldsymbol { \mathrm { Q M 9 } } ^ { 4 0 }$ data set for benchmarking. 
      QM9 contains l33 885 small organic molecules drawn from the                                                                                            
                                                                                                                                                             
