      2 \mathrm { d f } , \mathfrak { p } \big )$ level of theory. The data set was randomly split into a training set and a validation set of 113 885 and 10
      ooo molecules,respectively. The remaining 10 000 samples were used as a test set to evaluate the fitting results on QM9.                               
                                                                                                                                                             
      A more realistic and demanding test of the NN models is to train them on data sets of relatively small molecules and then test them on data sets of    
      larger and more diverse molecules, for which high-accuracy data are limited in number because of high computational cost.To this end, two data         
      sets,namely, OCELOT and AID,were selected to test the performance of X2-GNN trained on QM9 on unseen large molecules. The OCELOT (organic crystals in  
      electronic and light-oriented technologies)data set4142is a data setofcrystallineorganic semiconducting materials.We used the curated version of the   
      data set,42 which consists of the ${ \mathrm { S M I L E S } } ^ { 4 3 }$ strings of 25 251 organic $\pi$ -conjugated molecules.Many of these molecules
      have fused ring systems. The data set was filtered to exclude molecules containing elements other than HCNOF,resulting in a subset of $1 6 4 1 1$      
      molecules. Among these，16 166 molecules were successfully optimized using the same level of theory as QM9 using Gaussian $1 6 . ^ { 4 4 }$ The $U _ { 
      0 }$ values of the optimized molecules were collected to construct a test data set. The number of heavy atoms ranges from 7 to 75 for molecules in this
      challenging data set. More computational details are provided in section S4.AID1706 is a subset of the GEOM data set45 that contains drug-like         
      molecules with potential activity against SARS-Cov. From this data set， 5OO of the largest molecules (with ${ > } 3 4$ heavy atoms per molecule) were 
      selected, all containing only HCNOF elements. Of these selected molecules, 451 have undergone successful geometry optimization using a consistent      
      theoretical level， here collectively termed“AID".Figure 3 illustrates the distribution of the three data sets based on the number of non-hydrogen     
      atoms per molecule.                                                                                                                                    
                                                                                                                                                             
      SchNet,12 DimeNet++,21llgro,ndEqufoer2 were used as baseline models. These architectures are popular in applications such as the simulation of         
      molecular systems or materials， as well as in secondary development.46-48 Meanwhile， they represent state-of-the-art (SOTA） results for molecular   
      GNNs at various developmental stages. For example, SchNet is among the earliest GNNs for molecular systems that introduced continuous filter           
      convolutional layers to capture the interactions between atoms.DimeNet $^ { + + }$ isan enhanced version of DimeNet, which introduced angular          
      information between atom pairs, improving the model's ability to capture the geometric structure of molecules.Allegro is an equivariant model utilizing
      tensor representation,achieving the current SOTA results on various data sets.Building upon the principles of equivariance,Equiformer further          
      incorporated the attention mechanism, enhancing the model's ability to focus on relevant interactions and improving its performance. These baseline    
      models provide a robust comparison for evaluating the performance and capabilities of X2-GNN.                                                          
                                                                                                                                                             
      The test results are listed in Table 1. We report mean absolute errors (MAEs) for the test data.X2-GNN achieves an MAE of $6 . 7 6 \ \mathrm { m e V }$
      for QM9.Although this does not hit the SOTA results,it remains competitive compared to baseline models.We emphasize that the primary focus should be   
      the model performance on molecules of increasing complexity, bearing in mind that the so-called chemical accuracy is ${ \sim } 4 3$ meV $\left( 1      
      \mathrm { \ k c a l / m o l } \right) ,$ ,and the accumulated errors for the DFTlabeled data are often below chemical accuracy.2649 Encouragingly, for 
      the OCELOT data set, X2-GNN achieves a per-atom MAE of $5 . 2 4 ~ \mathrm { m e V }$ ，significantly outperforming other models. In the case of the AID
      data set, X2-GNN has a per-atom MAE of $3 . 3 6 ~ \mathrm { m e V } ,$ while other models，especially SchNet and Equiformer，exhibit notably larger    
      errors.Importantly，the consistent per-atom MAEs achieved by X2-GNN across both the OCELOT and AID data sets underscore its robustness in handling a   
      wide range of chemical contexts.                                                                                                                       
                                                                                                                                                             
