      The trends of per-atom MAE with respect to the system size for X2-GNN and other baseline models are illustrated in Figure 4.As depicted, for the       
      OCELOTand AID data sets, X2- GNN outperforms all baseline models in terms of both accuracy and stability.X2-GNN exhibits the fewest outliers, the most 
      densely clustered error distribution，and a more consistent error distribution as the size of molecules increases. In contrast, the baseline models    
      generally show a trend of an increasing error with larger molecules,particularly evident in the OCELOT data set,whereas X2-GNN maintains a stable      
      performance regardless of the molecular size.The results indicate that the generalization capability of GNNs to a more diverse chemical space cannot be
      directly inferred from tests on small molecule data sets,highlighting the need for more rigorous and comprehensive evaluations before these models are 
      deployed in practical applications.                                                                                                                    
                                                                                                                                                             
      To verify that checking the dependence of the integral features on the size of the basis sets and to ensure that the generalization ability of X2-GNN  
      on large molecules is not an incidental event， we re-implemented X2-GNN using four additional basis sets other than $6 { \cdot } 3 1 1 { \cdot } 4 ( 3
      \mathrm { d f } , 2 \mathrm { p } )$ . The selected basis sets are STO-3G, 6-31G(d),aug-cc-pVTZ,and aug-cc-$\mathrm { p V Q Z } ,$ representing the    
      tendency from the minimum basis set to a large quadruple- $\cdot \zeta$ one.All resulting models showed a similar performance，with the per-atom MAE on
      larger molecules converging as training progresses. The details are summarized in Table S3 in section S6. Figure S illustrates the per-heavy-atom error
      trends during the training progress for the aug-cc-pVTZ basis set. The model was trained with the QM9 data set,with $1 0 0 0 0$ molecules randomly     
      selected as validation data. After several training epochs，a model checkpoint was saved. Finally， the models predict the properties of all molecules 
      in the OCELOT data set, and a linear regression of the prediction error (per heavy atom) versus the number of heavy atoms in the molecule was          
      performed. The slope of the linear regresson function and the goodness of fit $\left( R ^ { 2 } \right)$ were recorded. An $R ^ { 2 }$ value           
      approaching 1.0 indicates a better fit, while a regression slope approaching 0.0 suggests that the prediction error does not vary with molecular       
      size.This suggests that X2-GNN exhibits robustness and has strong natural generalization ability, allowing it to be trained with a small molecule data 
      set and then to have subsequent proficient performance on larger molecules.                                                                            
                                                                                                                                                             
      An ablation study is conducted to help identify the improvement in the generalization ability contributed by each component of the proposed methods.As 
      shown in Table 2,the model using only the S matrix (abbreviated as the S model） shows a per-atom MAE close to that of the model utilizing both        
      matrices (abbreviated as the HS model) on QM9 and AID.This may indicate that the S integrals， containing both geometric information and element       
      composition information， serve as an effective local descriptor. They can empower the traditional radial expansion and atom embedding method,         
      providing a natural generalization ability rooted in quantum chemistry. Including $H ^ { \mathrm { c o r e } }$ can further boost the model's          
      predictive power on the OCELOT data set, although it seems not to be beneficial as a feature on its own.Both the model excluding the attention         
      mechanism and the model excluding the integral features show a relatively large per-atom MAE.This suggests that both the integral features and the     
      attention-based message passing algorithm are critical for building a transferable model.                                                              
                                                                                                                                                             
