      The key difference between AID and OCELOT data sets is that many molecules in the OCELOT data set are delocalized due to their conjugated              
      systems，which may not be fully captured by local descriptors alone. While the S integrals provide insight into the strength and nature of chemical    
      bonds between neighboring atoms, the $H ^ { \mathrm { c o r e } }$ integrals describe the movement of electrons under the interaction between electrons
      and nuclei, which helps in assessing the extent of electron delocalization within the molecule.From this point of view，we examined the error          
      distribution of the S model and the HS model across molecules in the OCELOT data set. Interestingly,we found that all molecules with an error of ${ > }
      1$ $\mathbf { k c a l } / \mathbf { m o l }$ $\mathrm { ( 4 3 . 4 ~ m e V ) }$ per atom for the S model are highly conjugated systems (Figure S2 in    
      section S7). The average peratomMAE for these molecules is reduced from $5 8 . 2 \ \mathrm { m e V }$ for the S model to $2 5 . 7 \mathrm { m e V }$   
      for the HS model. The latter is $5 5 . 8 \%$ smaller than the former, strongly supporting our hypothesis.                                              
                                                                                                                                                             
      To further validate this hypothesis,we tested the S model and the HS model on two types of typical conjugated molecules, i.e.， conjugated polyenes and
      polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs).For comparison, we also evaluated the behavior of the S model and the HS model on $n$ -alkanes.The results are 
      shown in Figure 6,and the corresponding molecules are listed in Figure S3 in section S7.While no significant difference was observed for alkanes, the  
      inclusion of the $H ^ { \mathrm { c o r e } }$ integral features greatly reduced the model's error on delocalized systems.On conjugated polyenes with  
      $_ { 4 - 2 0 }$ carbon atoms,the HS model consistently showed a small error regardless of the system size.In contrast，the MAE associated with the S   
      model gradually increased as the carbon chain lengthened.For PAHs, the HS model had a $5 3 . 7 \%$ lower per-atom MAE $\left( 2 3 . 0 { \mathrm { ~ m e
      V } } \right)$ compared to that of the S model $\left( 4 9 . 7 \ : \ : \mathrm { \ m e V } \right)$ . These experiments demonstrated that the inclusion
      of the $H ^ { \mathrm { c o r e } }$ integral features effectively improves the model performance on delocalized molecules.                            
                                                                                                                                                             
