      If the error introduced by model fiting were controlled to a relatively low level, further promotion in accuracy compared to the true values should    
      originate from the DFT labels.To this end, we tested whether X2-GNN can adapt to a more accurate doubly hybrid DFT level while maintaining the         
      transferability. To enable the model to predict doubly hybrid level properties, we calculated the XYGJ-OS/G3Large50,5i single-point energies for all   
      conformations in the original QM9 data set using $\mathrm { Q } \mathrm { - }$ Chem.52 The model trained on the original QM9 data set was then         
      fine-tuned on this new data set (termed QM9@XYGJ-OS) with the same data split as in the previous experiments.As XYGJ-OS calculations on large molecules
      are computationally intensive,a representative subset of the curated OCELOT was sampled using an adjusted Kennard-Stone (K-S） sampling algorithm53.54 
      to perform XYGJ-OS calculations (see Table S4 in section S8).After successful computations,a test data set with 3965 molecules was formed (termed      
      OCELOT@XYGJ-OS).                                                                                                                                       
                                                                                                                                                             
      The fine-tuned model achieved an MAE of $7 . 1 1 \ \mathrm { m e V }$ for the QM9@XYGJ-OS test set. On the OCELOT $\textcircled{2}$ XYGJ-OS data set,  
      it yielded an MAE of $0 . 2 9 7 { \mathrm { ~ e V } }$ and per-atom MAE of 7.90 meV. The error distribution and deviation trend on OCELOT $@$ XYGJ-OS  
      are shown in Figure 7. The MAE of X2-GNN on both QM9@XYGJ-OS and OCELOT@XYGJ-OS is slightly higher than that on the corresponding data sets at the     
      B3LYP/6-31G(2df,p）level.This may indicate that introducing new features that can describe better the information on unoccupied orbitals encoded in    
      doubly hybrid DFT methods is necessary. In general, the behavior of the current X2-GNN model on the XYGJ-OS data is already quite satisfactory. The    
      per-atom MAE remains consistent across different-sized molecules,and there is no significant systematic underestimation or overestimation of the       
      energies.                                                                                                                                              
                                                                                                                                                             
