      GNN achieves the best results on most of the metrics, verifying the effectiveness of the proposed S model and the X2-GNN framework in PES modeling.    
      Furthermore，X2-GNN can generate a conservative force，making it suitable for MD simulations.A torsion scan is shown in Figure S4 in section S9 to     
      demonstrate the smoothness of the modeled PES.                                                                                                         
                                                                                                                                                             
      The success in predicting total energies and PES modeling indicates that X2-GNN provides high-quality atom pair representations，which encourages us to
      apply X2-GNN to chemical bond property prediction. Specifically，we aim to predict the bond dissociation energy (BDE） directly from the representation
      of the dissociated bond in a molecule at its equilibrium position. The BDE reflects the strength of a chemical bond and is crucial for molecular       
      stability analysis and reaction mechanism prediction. Traditionally，BDE prediction involves optimizing and calculating the enthalpy change of the     
      molecule and its dissociated fragments.Direct BDE prediction from the molecule can eliminate the need for fragment calculations,which greatly simplifes
      the calculation process.                                                                                                                               
                                                                                                                                                             
      ![](images/163f4187d2eb807161c09ad89a6434d5382c360daea24ed8ca77088a15ba4d88.jpg)                                                                       
      Figure8.(a)BDE prediction results on the test set. (b） Demonstrationof the BDEdata preparation and prediction proceses                                
                                                                                                                                                             
      Several previous works have successfully predicted BDEs using GNNmodels9However,theavailabledatasetsin these studies provide only two-dimensional      
      molecular graphs and lack the optimized molecular structures required by X2- GNN.To make good use of the existing data, we constructed a BDE data set  
      based on QM9.For each molecule in QM9, every single bond was recorded and dissociated to form a bond dissociation reaction.Using all 133 885 molecules 
      in QM9,we constructed $1 0 7 7 8 7 2$ reactions. After sanitization and optimization of the radical fragments,a BDE data set with 741 321 distinct bond
      dissociation reactions derived from 128 926 molecules was created. The data set was randomly split into training,validation, and testing sets in a     
      ratio of 8:1:1. The calculation and refinement details are provided in section S10.                                                                    
                                                                                                                                                             
      For BDE prediction,we made several adjustments to X2- GNN's model architecture and training settings. First， we aggregated directed edge              
      representation into a bond representation,as the BDE is a property of chemical bonds. Specifically，a three-layer MLP is used to map the               
      representations of directed atom pairs corresponding to a dissociable bond to its BDE.Additionally, to regularize the learned bond representations,we  
      introduced a multitask loss to supervise the training process.In addition to predicting BDEs, the bond-wise representation and the atom-wise           
      representation were used to predict the enthalpy of the molecule (termed $H ^ { \mathrm { b o n d } }$ and $H ^ { \mathrm { a t o m } }$               
      ，respectively). As a consequence, the loss function consists of multiple parts:                                                                       
                                                                                                                                                             
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
      L _ { 1 } = \sum l _ { 1 } \mathrm { ( B D E _ { p r e d } , B D E _ { l a b e l } ) }                                                                 
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
      L _ { 2 } = \frac { l _ { 1 } ( H ^ { \mathrm { b o n d } } , H ^ { \mathrm { l a b e l } } ) + l _ { 1 } ( H ^ { \mathrm { a t o m } } , H ^ { \mathrm
      { l a b e l } } ) } { 2 }                                                                                                                              
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      where $l _ { 1 }$ represents the MAE loss function and $H ^ { \mathrm { l a b e l } }$ is the reference enthalpy of the molecule. $L _ { 1 }$ is the   
      major loss for training the BDE prediction blocks，while $L _ { 2 }$ serves as an auxiliary loss.The deviation between $H ^ { \mathrm { b o n d } }$   
      and $H ^ { \mathrm { a t o m } }$ is expected to be zero,which leads to another auxiliary part of the loss function:                                   
                                                                                                                                                             
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
      L _ { 3 } = l _ { 1 } ( H ^ { \mathrm { b o n d } } , H ^ { \mathrm { a t o m } } )                                                                    
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
