      # 2.2.1 KA-GCN Model                                                                                                                                   
                                                                                                                                                             
      Our KA-GCN architecture has three major steps, including node embedding, message-passing,and readout. First,one KAN layer is employed in the           
      initialization of node embeddings $h _ { v } ^ { ( 0 ) } , v \in V$ as follows,                                                                        
                                                                                                                                                             
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
      h _ { v } ^ { ( 0 ) } : = \mathrm { K A N } \left( f _ { v } \oplus ( \frac { 1 } { | N ( v ) | } \sum _ { u \in N ( v ) } f _ { u v } ) \right) ,     
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      vhere $N ( v )$ represents the set of the neighboring nodes of node $v$ (excluding $v$ itself), $\left| N ( v ) \right|$ is the number of ieighboring  
      nodes, and $\oplus$ represents vector concatenation.                                                                                                   
                                                                                                                                                             
      Second, one or several KAN layers are employed in the message passing module. At the layer $l$ , the KAN-based message passing module can be expressed 
      as,                                                                                                                                                    
                                                                                                                                                             
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
      h _ { v } ^ { ( l + 1 ) } : = h _ { v } ^ { ( l ) } + \mathrm { K A N } \left( h _ { v } ^ { ( l ) } \oplus \big ( \frac { 1 } { | N ( v ) | } \sum _ {
      u \in N ( v ) } h _ { u } ^ { ( l ) } \big ) \right) .                                                                                                 
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      Computationally, the first KAN layer maps initial feature vector $f _ { v }$ from dimension 113 to updated feature vector $h _ { v }$ vith dimension   
      64. After that, the feature dimension willstay the same in the later message-passing process.                                                          
                                                                                                                                                             
      Third, one or several KAN layers are employed in the readout module. After $L$ -th the message passing phases, we apply average pooling across all node
      features to obtain a latent vector for the molecular graph $G$ .An one-layer or two-layer KAN model is employed in readout module for the final        
      prediction as follows,                                                                                                                                 
                                                                                                                                                             
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
      \hat { y } : = \mathrm { K A N } \left( \frac { 1 } { | V | } \sum _ { v \in V } h _ { v } ^ { ( L ) } \right) \mathrm { o r } , \hat { y } : = \mathrm
      { K A N } \left( \mathrm { K A N } \left( \frac { 1 } { | V | } \sum _ { v \in V } h _ { v } ^ { ( L ) } \right) \right)                               
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      The cross-entropy is used as the loss function as follows,                                                                                             
                                                                                                                                                             
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
      \mathcal { L } : = - \sum _ { i } \left( y _ { i } \log ( \hat { y } _ { i } ) + ( 1 - y _ { i } ) \log ( 1 - \hat { y } _ { i } ) \right) ,           
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      where $y _ { i }$ denotes the actual label, and $\hat { y } _ { i }$ denotes the predicted label.                                                      
                                                                                                                                                             
      # 2.2.2KA-GAT Model                                                                                                                                    
                                                                                                                                                             
      Similar to the KA-GCNarchitecture,the KA-GATmodelinitiaizes tenodeandedgeembdings, $h _ { v } ^ { ( 0 ) }$ and $h _ { u v } ^ { ( 0 ) }$ ， using a KAN
      layer as follows:                                                                                                                                      
                                                                                                                                                             
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
      \begin{array} { r l } & { h _ { v } ^ { ( 0 ) } : = { \mathrm { K A N } } \left( f _ { v } \oplus \left( \displaystyle \frac { 1 } { | N ( v ) | } \sum
      _ { u \in N ( v ) } f _ { u v } \right) \right) , } \\ & { h _ { u v } ^ { ( 0 ) } : = { \mathrm { K A N } } \left( { \mathrm { L i n e a r } } ( f _ {
      v } ) + { \mathrm { L i n e a r } } ( f _ { u v } ) + { \mathrm { L i n e a r } } ( f _ { u } ) \right) } \end{array}                                  
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      Subsequently,the KA-GAT model uses these initial embeddings to compute atention scores that guide the aggregation of messages:                         
                                                                                                                                                             
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
      \begin{array} { r l } & { z _ { v } ^ { ( l + 1 ) } : = \mathrm { L i n e a r } \left( h _ { v } ^ { ( l ) } \right) , z _ { u } ^ { ( l + 1 ) } : =   
      \mathrm { L i n e a r } \left( h _ { u } ^ { ( l ) } \right) , } \\ & { \quad \alpha _ { u v } : = \mathrm { S o f t m a x } \left( h _ { u v } ^ { ( l
      ) } \right) , m _ { u v } ^ { ( l + 1 ) } : = z _ { u } ^ { ( l + 1 ) } \cdot \alpha _ { u v } } \\ & { \quad \quad m _ { v } ^ { ( l + 1 ) } : = z _ {
      v } ^ { ( l + 1 ) } + \displaystyle \frac { 1 } { | N ( v ) | } \left( \displaystyle \sum _ { u \in N ( v ) } m _ { u v } ^ { ( l + 1 ) } \right) }    
      \end{array}                                                                                                                                            
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      Then,one or more KAN layers are used to update the node and edge features:                                                                             
                                                                                                                                                             
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
      h _ { v } ^ { ( l + 1 ) } : = \mathbf { K A N } ( m _ { v } ^ { ( l + 1 ) } ) , \quad h _ { u v } ^ { ( l + 1 ) } : = \mathbf { K A N } ( h _ { u v } ^
      { ( l ) } )                                                                                                                                            
      $$                                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      Finally,after message passing, the KA-GAT model employs the same readout module as used in the KA-GCN.                                                 
                                                                                                                                                             
      # 2.3KA-GNNs for Molecular Property Analysis                                                                                                           
                                                                                                                                                             
      # 2.3.1Performance and comparison of KA-GNNs                                                                                                           
                                                                                                                                                             
      To evaluate the performance of our proposed KA-GNN models, we consider seven widely-used benchmark datasets from MoleculeNet [47]. Of                  
      these,threedatasets—MUV,HIV,and BACE—are from the biophysics domain.The rest four datasets are from the physiology domain, including BBBP, Tox21,      
      SIDER,and ClinTox.                                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                                                             
      In our comparative analysis across seven datasets, we evaluated a range of geometric deep learning models, including                                   
      AttentiveFP[48],D-MPNN[49],Mol-GDL[28],N-Gram[50],PretrainGNN[51],GROVER [26],GraphMVP[52], MolCLR [53],GEM [54], Uni-mol [55],SMPT[56],and            
      GNN-SKAN[38]. Additionally, we included our KAN-based GNN models,such as the GNN-SKAN [38].Further details on these models can be found in Subsection  
      Baseline Models.                                                                                                                                       
                                                                                                                                                             
      The comparative results in Table 2 further confrm the superiority of the KA-GNNs model. Our model achieves state-of-the-art performance acrosall       
      benchmark datasets, particularly excelling on complex and challenging datasets such as ClinTox and MUV.These results demonstrate the robust capability 
      of our model in handling molecular data. Notably, in the BBBP dataset, the AUC for KA-GCN and KA-GAT showed improvements of approximately $7 . 9 5 \%$ 
      and $7 . 6 8 \%$                                                                                                                                       
                                                                                                                                                             
