      achieving state-of-the-art on 7 molecular property prediction benchmarks., keyword: Kolmogorov-Arnold Network, Fourier series, Graph Neural Network,   
      Molecular Property Prediction, Message Passing, content: {'background': 'Graph neural networks (GNNs) have demonstrated enormous power in molecular    
      data analysis as key models in geometric deep learning. Traditional GNNs use Multi-layer Perceptrons (MLPs) with fixed activation functions, which     
      limits their expressiveness and interpretability. Recently, Kolmogorov-Arnold Networks (KANs) emerged as a promising alternative to MLPs, employing    
      learnable activation functions on edges rather than fixed activations on nodes. KANs eliminate traditional linear weight matrices and enhance model    
      accuracy and efficiency, particularly in solving partial differential equations. However, existing KAN-based GNN models are trivial—they only replace  
      the MLP in the readout part with a standard KAN module, missing opportunities to optimize the full GNN architecture. The motivation is to develop a    
      comprehensive KAN integration that optimizes GNN at all three major levels: node embedding, message passing, and readout, while providing theoretical  
      guarantees for approximation capability.', 'method': "The KA-GNN framework introduces three key innovations:\n\n1. **Fourier Series-Based KAN**:       
      Instead of B-splines, the authors use Fourier series as pre-activation functions: φ(x) = Σ(A_k·cos(kx) + B_k·sin(kx)), where A_k and B_k are learnable 
      parameters initialized from N(0, 1/(n_{l+1}×K)). This provides stronger approximation capability based on Carleson's theorem extension (Fefferman's    
      proof) for multivariable Fourier series convergence. Theorem 2 proves that for any f∈L²([0,2π]^n) and ε>0, there exists a Fourier-based KAN with K     
      harmonics such that the approximation error is less than ε.\n\n2. **Three-Level KAN Integration**:\n- **Node Embedding**: h_v^(0) = KAN(f_v ⊕ (1/|N(v)|
      Σ f_uv)), concatenating atom features with averaged edge features\n- **Message Passing**: h_v^(l+1) = h_v^(l) + KAN(h_v^(l) ⊕ (1/|N(v)| Σ h_u^(l))),   
      with residual connection\n- **Readout**: ŷ = KAN((1/|V| Σ h_v^(L))) for final prediction\n\n3. **Molecular Graph Construction**: Unlike traditional    
      covalent-bond-only graphs, KA-GNN includes both covalent bonds and non-covalent interactions (cutoff 5Å), enabling capture of more molecular           
      properties. Atom features are 92-dimensional (atomic number, radius, electronegativity one-hot encoded). Edge features are 21-dimensional including    
      bond directionality, type, length, and ring membership.\n\n**KA-GAT Variant**: Extends to attention-based message passing with attention scores        
      computed from edge embeddings, followed by KAN-based feature updates for both nodes and edges.\n\n**Why This Design Works**: Fourier series provide    
      universal approximation for L² functions, offering better expressiveness than B-splines for capturing periodic patterns in chemical structures. The    
      three-level integration ensures KAN's learnable activations benefit all stages of information flow, not just the final prediction.", 'experiment':     
      '**Datasets**: Seven benchmark datasets from MoleculeNet:\n- Biophysics: MUV (93,808 molecules, 17 tasks), HIV (41,127 molecules, 1 task), BACE (1,513 
      molecules, 1 task)\n- Physiology: BBBP (2,039 molecules, 1 task), Tox21 (7,831 molecules, 12 tasks), SIDER (1,427 molecules, 27 tasks), ClinTox (1,478 
      molecules, 2 tasks)\n\n**Baselines**: 13 geometric deep learning models including D-MPNN, AttentiveFP, N-Gram, PretrainGNN, GROVER, GraphMVP, MolCLR,  
      GEM, Mol-GDL, Uni-mol, SMPT, and GNN-SKAN.\n\n**Main Results**:\n- KA-GCN achieves best on BACE (0.890), BBBP (0.787), ClinTox (0.989), SIDER (0.842), 
      Tox21 (0.799), HIV (0.821)\n- KA-GAT achieves best on ClinTox (0.991), SIDER (0.847), HIV (0.823), MUV (0.834)\n- Notable improvements: ~7.95% AUC     
      improvement on BBBP vs baselines\n\n**Ablation Study**: Compared three base functions (B-spline, polynomial, Fourier) on both KA-GCN and KA-GAT across 
      all 7 datasets:\n- Fourier consistently outperforms B-spline and polynomial\n- Example on BACE: Fourier 0.890 vs Polynomial 0.853 vs B-spline 0.771    
      (KA-GCN)\n- Example on MUV: Fourier 0.834 vs Polynomial 0.787 vs B-spline 0.638 (KA-GCN)\n\n**Efficiency**: Fourier-based KAN significantly outperforms
      B-spline KAN in computational efficiency (Figure 2 shows ~2-3x speedup across datasets).\n\n**Comparison with Traditional GNNs**: KA-GCN vs GCN and    
      KA-GAT vs GAT on same molecular graphs shows consistent improvements (e.g., BBBP: GCN 0.735 vs KA-GCN 0.787).', 'result': "**Main Findings**:\n1.      
      **State-of-the-art Performance**: KA-GNNs achieve best or second-best results on all 7 benchmarks, demonstrating robust capability in molecular        
      property prediction.\n2. **Fourier Superiority**: Fourier-based KAN consistently outperforms B-spline and polynomial alternatives in both accuracy and 
      computational efficiency.\n3. **Comprehensive Integration**: Three-level KAN integration (node embedding, message passing, readout) outperforms trivial
      single-level replacement.\n4. **Non-covalent Interactions**: Including edges from non-covalent interactions (5Å cutoff) enhances molecular structure   
      understanding.\n\n**Principles Behind Performance**:\n- Fourier series' strong approximation capability for L² functions enables better capture of     
      periodicity and complexity in chemical structures\n- Learnable activation functions provide more expressive power than fixed activations\n-            
      Non-covalent edges capture more molecular properties than covalent-only graphs\n\n**Limitations**:\n1. **Interpretability Gap**: Despite KAN's design  
      for interpretability, the pruning process does not generate biologically meaningful superposition structures that are easy to interpret\n2.            
      **Computational Overhead**: While more efficient than B-spline KAN, Fourier KAN still has more parameters than standard MLPs\n\n**Future               
      Directions**:\n- Improve interpretability of learned Fourier coefficients\n- Explore other orthogonal basis functions (wavelets, Legendre              
      polynomials)\n- Extend to 3D molecular conformations and equivariant architectures", 'github_url': 'N/A', 'dataset': 'MoleculeNet benchmarks: MUV, HIV,
      BACE, BBBP, Tox21, SIDER, ClinTox', 'topic_relation': "This paper directly relates to the 'Advanced Algorithm Improvement' hypothesis in               
