      **Physical Pooling Functions** (arXiv 2207.13779): Pooling function selection should match property type—sum for extensive (size-dependent), mean/max  
      for intensive. Tg is intensive.\n2. **Equivariant Benchmark** (arXiv 2008.08461): Equivariant layers provide 23% error reduction vs 4% from            
      depth—parameter-efficient architectural innovation.\n3. **ENINet** (pool): Line graphs capture many-body equivariant interactions at triplet level,    
      addressing vanishing directional information.\n4. **SimPoly** (pool): Polymer-specific MLFF achieving Tg MAE=43K; inter-chain interactions essential;  
      cutoff ~6.5Å required.\n\nAll papers saved to memory (mem_086-089).\n\n**Experiment Conducted:**\nExecuted IAPT v2 (Intensive-Aware Polymer            
      Transformer) integrating Days 1-4 insights: attention-based pooling for intensive Tg property, L1 equivariant features, line graph for angular         
      relationships, monomer conformer features. Ran comprehensive ablation study with 6 variants.\n\n**Results:**\n- **Test RMSE: 35.08** (5-seed ensemble) 
      vs **SOTA 30.8** (PerioGT)\n- **Gap: 4.28 RMSE points** — FAILED to beat SOTA\n- Day 1 result (34.14) remains best\n\n**Ablation Results:**\n| Variant 
      | RMSE | Key Finding |\n|---------|------|-------------|\n| Mean pooling | 37.89 | WORST — validates intensive property insight |\n| Max pooling |     
      35.35 | Good |\n| Attention pooling | **35.19** | BEST single model |\n| No equivariant | 36.76 | Removing hurts ~1.1 RMSE |\n| No line graph | 36.43 |
      Removing hurts ~0.5 RMSE |\n\n**Validated Hypotheses:**\n1. **Intensive property insight CONFIRMED**: Mean pooling worst for Tg (intensive),           
      attention/max best\n2. **Equivariant features help**: ~1.1 RMSE improvement\n3. **Line graph helps**: ~0.5 RMSE improvement\n\n**Critical Issues:**\n1.
      **Conformer generation failure**: 83% failure rate for polymer trimers (unusual atom types Cd, Pb, Zn), losing 3D geometric information\n2.            
      **Overfitting**: Train RMSE ~12-17 vs Val RMSE ~35-37\n3. **Missing pre-training**: PerioGT's SOTA uses PI1M pretraining—we haven't used this          
      yet\n\n**Key Insight:**\nArchitecture innovations (intensive-aware pooling, equivariant features, line graphs) are validated but insufficient.         
      **Pre-training on PI1M is likely the critical missing piece**—PerioGT achieves 30.8 via pretraining.\n\n**Recommendations for Day 5:**\n1. Pre-training
      on PI1M dataset (critical missing piece)\n2. Fix conformer generation: use monomers instead of trimers\n3. Address overfitting with better             
      regularization\n4. Consider hierarchical architecture for multi-scale polymer structure"}, {'day': 5, 'journal': '**Day 5 Journal: Pre-training        
      Experiment - Hypothesis Disproven**\n\n**Papers Selected and Analyzed:**\nSelected 4 papers following MetaMethod criteria (2 from pool, 2 from         
      arXiv):\n1. **GPIP** (pool): Geometry-enhanced SSL with 3 tasks (masking, denoising, contrastive learning) achieving 10x data efficiency\n2. **MoCL**  
      (arXiv): Domain knowledge-aware contrastive learning with bioisostere substitution for semantics-preserving augmentations\n3. **GraphMAE** (arXiv):    
      Masked graph autoencoding with Scaled Cosine Error, SOTA on MoleculeNet transfer learning\n4. **GNN-SSM** (pool): Unified theory of GNN pathologies    
      (gradient vanishing, over-smoothing, over-squashing) with state-space model solution\n\nAll papers saved to memory (mem_092-095).\n\n**Experiment      
      Conducted:**\nTested the Day 4 hypothesis that "pre-training on PI1M is the critical missing piece." Implemented GraphMAE-style masked autoencoding    
      combined with MoCL-style domain-aware augmentations. Ran 2 iterations with 100K and 300K PI1M samples respectively.\n\n**Results: HYPOTHESIS           
      DISPROVEN**\n| Iteration | Test RMSE | Target (SOTA) | Gap |\n|-----------|-----------|---------------|-----|\n| v1 (100K PI1M) | 35.60 | 30.8 | +4.8  
      |\n| v2 (300K PI1M) | 36.14 | 30.8 | +5.34 |\n\n**Best Day 5 result: RMSE 35.60** — worse than Day 1\'s 34.14. Pre-training did NOT help.\n\n**Key     
      Findings (Negative Result):**\n1. **Pre-training alone insufficient**: GraphMAE-style masked feature reconstruction on PI1M did not improve Tg         
      prediction\n2. **Objective mismatch**: SCE loss collapsed to 0.0003, suggesting reconstruction task misaligned with downstream Tg prediction\n3.       
      **Overfitting persists**: Train RMSE ~22 vs Val RMSE ~35\n4. **Architecture > Pre-training**: PerioGT\'s SOTA (30.8) likely comes from                 
      periodicity-aware architecture (multimer graphs, periodic edges), not just pre-training\n\n**Recommendations for Day 6:**\n1. Focus on architecture    
      innovations (implement PerioGT\'s periodicity-aware design) rather than pre-training\n2. Apply GNN-SSM insights for gradient flow and overfitting      
