      {'background': "Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) operating via message passing suffer from three fundamental limitations as depth increases: (1) feature   
      collapse/over-smoothing, where node representations become exponentially similar; (2) over-squashing, where information from distant nodes is          
      compressed into fixed-size vectors; (3) vanishing gradients, hindering training. While over-smoothing and over-squashing have been studied extensively,
      no unified theoretical framework explains their common underlying cause. The RNN literature has extensively studied vanishing gradients, but this      
      problem has been surprisingly overlooked in GNNs. This work bridges the gap between recurrent and graph learning by analyzing GNNs from a sequence     
      model perspective, revealing that the normalized adjacency matrix introduces additional contraction beyond what RNNs experience, causing what the      
      authors term 'extreme gradient vanishing'. This unified view explains why existing solutions (residual connections, normalization) work and provides   
      principled approaches for designing better architectures.", 'method': "The core technical contribution is GNN-SSM, a state-space model formulation of  
      GNNs: H^(k+1)⊤ = ΛH^(k)⊤ + BX^(k)⊤, where Λ is the state transition matrix, B is the input matrix, and F_θ(H^(k), k) is a time-varying coupling        
      function (any MPNN layer). Key theoretical findings: (1) Theorem 3.2 shows that Jacobian singular values are modulated by squared eigenvalues of       
      normalized adjacency, causing extreme gradient vanishing since |λ_i| ≤ 1; (2) Proposition 3.3 demonstrates that the Jacobian spectrum is controlled    
      through eigenvalues of Λ, with eig(Λ) ≈ 1 bringing the system to 'edge of chaos'; (3) Proposition 4.4 proves that contractive GNN layers decrease      
      Dirichlet energy, linking vanishing gradients to over-smoothing through Lipschitz constants; (4) Theorem 5.1 shows over-squashing sensitivity is       
      controlled by both topology and model dynamics. The GNN-SSM construction allows Λ and B to be fixed (not trained) to guarantee desired spectral        
      properties, while only F_θ is optimized. The framework is agnostic to the coupling function, allowing any MPNN layer to be used. For Λ construction, a 
      random unitary matrix is generated and scaled to control spectral radius, enabling precise control over memory dynamics without losing                 
      permutation-equivariance.", 'experiment': 'Experiments conducted on: (1) Cora dataset for node classification with varying layer depths; (2) Synthetic 
      analysis of Dirichlet energy evolution, latent vector norms, and Jacobian eigenvalue spectra; (3) Long-range graph benchmark (LRGB) datasets including 
      PascalVOC-SP, Peptides-func, Peptides-struct, and COCO-SP. Baselines compared: GCN, GAT, GIN, Gated-GCN, GraphSAGE, and various GNN-SSM variants. Key  
      experimental configurations: (a) Analysis of Jacobian eigenvalue distributions showing GCN/GAT have long tails near zero; (b) Dirichlet energy         
      evolution with different ||Λ||_2 values demonstrating controlled collapse rates; (c) Node classification accuracy degradation with depth for           
      traditional GNNs vs. stability of GNN-SSM; (d) Long-range tasks showing GNN-SSM with graph rewiring (DIGL) outperforms baselines. Ablation studies     
      examined: effect of Λ spectral radius on feature evolution, comparison of SSM-only vs. GNN-SSM, and combination with graph rewiring techniques. The    
      experiments validate that: (i) traditional GNNs experience extreme gradient vanishing even after few layers; (ii) GNN-SSM with eig(Λ) ≈ 1 maintains    
      stable Dirichlet energy and performance with depth; (iii) combining gradient mitigation with graph rewiring is most effective for long-range           
      modeling.', 'result': 'Main findings: (1) Traditional GNNs (GCN, GAT) are by design prone to extreme gradient vanishing due to normalized adjacency    
      contraction—the Jacobian singular values are scaled by squared adjacency eigenvalues, causing faster spectrum collapse than RNNs or MLPs; (2)          
      Over-smoothing is directly linked to vanishing gradients through Lipschitz constants—contractive layers cause node features to collapse to zero fixed  
      point, minimizing Dirichlet energy; (3) GNN-SSM with eig(Λ) ≈ 1 maintains stable performance across depths, validating the edge-of-chaos principle from
      RNN literature; (4) Long-range modeling requires both graph rewiring (topology) and vanishing gradient mitigation (dynamics)—addressing only one is    
      insufficient. On long-range benchmarks, GNN-SSM combined with DIGL rewiring achieves state-of-the-art on PascalVOC-SP and competitive results on       
      Peptides datasets. The framework provides a diagnostic tool: analyzing layer-wise Jacobian eigenvalues reveals whether a GNN will over-smooth or train 
      at large depth. Limitations: the simple fixed Λ scheme, while effective, could be extended with learnable gating mechanisms; the analysis focuses      
      primarily on GCN/GAT architectures. Future directions include incorporating learnable state transitions and exploring connections to other deep        
      learning stability techniques.', 'github_url': 'N/A', 'dataset': 'Cora, PascalVOC-SP, Peptides-func, Peptides-struct, COCO-SP (LRGB benchmark)',       
      'topic_relation': "This paper addresses fundamental GNN limitations (vanishing gradients, over-smoothing, over-squashing) that directly impact polymer 
