      'connections': {}}, {'id': 'mem_093', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'MoCL introduces domain knowledge-aware contrastive learning for molecular      
      graphs via two innovations: (1) substructure substitution using bioisosteres (230 rules) for semantics-preserving augmentation, and (2) global contrast
      loss using ECFP-derived molecular similarity to encode dataset structure, achieving state-of-the-art on 7 molecular benchmarks.', 'keyword':           
      'contrastive learning, molecular graph, domain knowledge, bioisostere, pretraining', 'content': {'background': "Graph neural networks (GNNs) have      
      achieved state-of-the-art performance on graph-related tasks including drug-related problems in the biomedical domain. However, like most deep learning
      architectures, GNNs require large amounts of labeled data, while task-specific labels in real-world biomedical applications (e.g., drug responses from 
      biological experiments) are expensive and time-consuming to obtain. Self-supervised pretraining on GNNs has been actively explored, with contrastive   
      learning emerging as a promising approach by maximizing mutual information between paired graph augmentations. However, current graph contrastive      
      learning frameworks face two critical limitations: (1) Augmentations designed for general graphs (node dropping, edge perturbation, subgraph           
      extraction, attribute masking) may not be suitable for molecular graphs—random perturbations can alter molecular semantics (e.g., dropping a carbon    
      atom in aspirin's phenyl ring breaks the aromatic system, resulting in an alkene chain). (2) The contrastive scheme only learns representations        
      invariant to local perturbations, neglecting the global structure of the dataset where some graphs should be closer in embedding space due to          
      structural similarity. This work addresses these limitations by infusing domain knowledge at both local and global levels specifically for molecular   
      graphs.", 'method': 'MoCL proposes a double contrast objective integrating local-level and global-level domain knowledge:\n\n**Local-level Domain      
      Knowledge (Substructure Substitution)**:\nUnlike general augmentations that randomly perturb nodes/edges, MoCL introduces bioisostere-based            
      augmentation where valid substructures are replaced by bioisosteres—molecular fragments with similar physical/chemical properties. The authors compiled
      218 substitution rules from domain resources plus 12 additional rules for general carbon groups (230 total). Rules are represented as SMARTS strings   
      (e.g., carboxylic acid → nitrogen heterocycle). The 36 unique source substructures are categorized into 8 groups: CA (carboxylic acid), Ester, Ketone, 
      Phenyl, Tbutyl, dsAmide, msAmide, nsAmide. Key advantage: variation is introduced without altering molecular properties. Multiple substitutions can be 
      applied for diversity, with rule availability updated after each round.\n\n**Global-level Domain Knowledge**:\nMolecular similarity is calculated using
      Extended Connectivity Fingerprints (ECFPs) with Tanimoto coefficient: s(e₁, e₂) = N₁₂/(N₁ + N₂ - N₁₂). Two strategies are proposed:\n1. Direct         
      supervision: Least square loss between predicted and target similarity\n2. Contrastive objective: Similar graph pairs have higher mutual information vs
      background\n\n**Overall Framework**:\n1. Generate two augmented views G¹, G² from original graph G using substructure substitution\n2. Feed G¹, G², G  
      through GNN encoder f → representations h¹, h², h\n3. Apply projection head g → embeddings z¹, z², z\n4. Optimize: L = L^local +                       
      λL^global\n\n**Theoretical Justification**:\nLemma 1 shows the objective is equivalent to triplet loss in metric learning, pulling positive pairs close
      while pushing negative pairs apart from both local and global perspectives.', 'experiment': '**Datasets**: 7 benchmark molecular datasets covering     
      diverse tasks:\n- bace (1513 molecules, binding results with human proteins)\n- bbbp (2050 molecules, blood-brain barrier penetration)\n- clintox (1483
      molecules, FDA clinical trial toxicity)\n- mutag (188 molecules, mutagenic effects)\n- sider (1427 molecules, adverse drug reactions)\n- tox21 (7831   
      molecules, in vitro toxicity screening)\n- toxcast (8597 molecules, 617 tasks, toxicity assays)\n\n**Evaluation Protocols**:\n1. Linear protocol: Fix  
      pretrained representations, train linear classifier\n2. Semi-supervised protocol: Sample ~100 molecules for training, finetune all layers\n\n**Model   
      Architecture**: GIN (3 layers, 512-dim hidden, 128-dim embedding) with edge features. Projection head: 2-layer MLP. Cosine similarity for contrastive  
      loss.\n\n**Baselines**:\n- Vanilla GNN (scratch training)\n- Self-supervised: InfoGraph, EdgePred, ContextPred, Masking\n- Graph contrastive learning: 
      node dropping, edge perturbation, subgraph extraction, attribute masking\n- MICRO-Graph (motif-based contrastive)\n\n**Implementation**: Pretrain 100  
      epochs, batch size 32, Adam optimizer (lr=0.001), dropout 0.5. Augmentation ratio 0.2 for general augmentations, augmentation times {1,2,3,5} for      
      MoCL-DK. λ ∈ {0.5, 1.0, 5.0, 10.0} for global loss. ECFP dimension 1024 for similarity calculation.', 'result': '**Main Results**:\nMoCL-DK (local     
      knowledge only) outperforms all general augmentations and self-supervised baselines on most datasets. MoCL-DK-G (with global knowledge) further        
      improves performance. Best results achieved by combining MoCL-DK with attribute masking.\n\n**Linear Protocol (AUC)**:\n- MoCL-DK: bace 0.801, bbbp    
      0.870, clintox 0.727, mutag 0.950, sider 0.615, tox21 0.740, toxcast 0.636\n- MoCL+AttrMask: bace 0.831, bbbp 0.892, clintox 0.695, mutag 0.947, sider 
      0.623, tox21 0.768, toxcast 0.653\n- MoCL-DK-G(LS): bace 0.831, bbbp 0.892, clintox 0.724, mutag 0.958, sider 0.623, tox21 0.777, toxcast              
      0.659\n\n**Semi-supervised Protocol**: Consistent improvements over baselines, demonstrating effectiveness when labels are scarce.\n\n**Ablation       
      Studies**:\n1. Augmentation combinations: MoCL-DK + attribute masking performs best, validating complementary nature of semantics-preserving and       
      attribute-based augmentations.\n2. Global loss strategies: Contrastive objective outperforms direct supervision (least square loss).\n3.               
      Hyperparameters: λ=1.0, neighbor size ~10 work well; performance degrades with too large λ or neighbor size.\n\n**Key Insights**:\n1. Domain-aware     
      augmentation preserves molecular semantics better than random perturbation\n2. Global similarity knowledge enriches representations with dataset       
      structure\n3. Bioisostere rules are chemically meaningful and transferable\n4. Theoretical connection to triplet loss explains                         
      effectiveness\n\n**Limitations**:\n1. Substitution rules require domain expertise to compile\n2. Rules may not cover all molecular substructures\n3.   
      Global similarity calculation requires external resources (ECFP)\n4. Transfer learning to other domains needs further study', 'github_url':            
      'https://github.com/illidanlab/MoCL-DK', 'dataset': 'bace, bbbp, clintox, mutag, sider, tox21, toxcast (MoleculeNet benchmarks)', 'topic_relation':    
      "This paper directly addresses the 'Self-Supervised Pretraining' hypothesis in TopicState. Day 4 journal identified pre-training on PI1M as the        
      critical missing piece to beat SOTA (current best 34.14 vs SOTA 30.8). MoCL provides a methodology for contrastive learning on molecular graphs that   
      could be adapted for polymer pre-training.", 'topic_contribution': "1. **Actionable for PI1M pre-training**: MoCL's substructure substitution approach 
      can be adapted for polymer SMILES—the bioisostere concept could be extended to polymer repeat units (e.g., substituting monomer backbones while        
      preserving polymer properties).\n\n2. **Semantics-preserving augmentation**: Day 1 analysis showed 83% conformer generation failure for polymer        
      trimers. MoCL's 2D augmentation approach avoids 3D conformer issues entirely—directly applicable to polymer SMILES.\n\n3. **Global similarity for      
      polymers**: ECFP similarity can be applied to polymer fingerprints; alternatively, polymer-specific similarity metrics (e.g., monomer composition,     
      chain length) could encode polymer global structure.\n\n4. **Design decision for Day 5+**: When implementing pre-training on PI1M, use MoCL-style      
      domain-aware augmentation rather than random perturbation. Consider polymer-specific substructures (e.g., backbone vs side chain substitutions).",     
      'local_path': 'papers/day_5/2106.04509/2106.04509.md'}, 'connections': {}}, {'id': 'mem_092', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'GPIP proposes a        
      geometry-enhanced self-supervised pretraining framework for machine learning interatomic potentials (MLIPs) that leverages inexpensive classical       
      molecular dynamics (CMD) data with three complementary SSL tasks (masking, denoising, contrastive learning), achieving 10x data efficiency improvement 
      with negligible computational cost compared to ab initio calculations.', 'keyword': 'self-supervised pretraining, interatomic potentials,              
      geometry-enhanced learning, data efficiency, molecular dynamics', 'content': {'background': 'Machine learning interatomic potentials (MLIPs) are       
      crucial for molecular dynamics simulations, achieving ab initio accuracy with high efficiency. However, high-performance MLIPs require large amounts of
      labeled data from expensive ab initio calculations (density functional theory). Existing pretraining approaches for interatomic potentials rely on     
      existing datasets like OC20 and ANI-1, which are costly to generate and have limited generalization to new molecular systems. The key bottleneck is the
      scarcity of training data due to expensive ab initio calculations. This work addresses the question: Can we leverage inexpensive unlabeled structural  
      data to improve MLIPs? The motivation is to develop a scalable pretraining framework that uses classical molecular dynamics (CMD) simulations with     
      empirical force fields to generate abundant unlabeled configurations, then apply self-supervised learning to extract structural information before     
      fine-tuning on limited labeled ab initio data.', 'method': 'GPIP (Geometry-enhanced Pretraining on Interatomic Potentials) consists of two stages: (1) 
      geometric structure generation via CMD simulations with empirical force fields (UFF, OPLS-AA), and (2) geometry-enhanced self-supervised pretraining   
      with three complementary tasks. **Task 1: Restore masked atoms with noisy coordinates.** A subset of nodes is masked with [MASK] token, and the GNN    
      encoder converts the masked conformation to latent codes. Small noise is added to coordinates to prevent trivial solutions from atom-type information  
      leakage via 3D coordinates. A re-mask technique replaces masked node features in latent codes with [DMASK] token. The decoder reconstructs original    
      node features using scaled cosine error. This task captures topology information indicating atomic connectivity. **Task 2: Predict noise with masked   
      atoms.** Random Gaussian noise ε is added to coordinates to create perturbed coordinates R̂ = R + ε. The model predicts the noise ε rather than         
      unperturbed coordinates R (since GNNs with skip connections can easily restore R). To increase difficulty, a proportion of atoms are masked. The       
      objective minimizes ||f_θ(X̃, R̂) - ε||². This task extracts spatial structure information. **Task 3: Contrastive learning with 3D network.** A 3D       
      network encodes pairwise Euclidean distances mapped to higher-dimensional space using sine/cosine functions. The same molecular configuration with     
      perturbed coordinates is fed into a GNN to generate latent codes. The mutual information between GNN and 3D network outputs is maximized, forcing the  
      GNN to learn 3D structural information. The final loss combines all three tasks: L_final = L_mask + αL_denoise + βL_contrast. GPIP is model-agnostic   
      and works with both invariant (SchNet, DimeNet) and equivariant (GemNet-T, PaiNN, EGNN, SphereNet) GNN architectures.', 'experiment': 'GPIP is         
      evaluated on four benchmark datasets spanning small molecules to complex periodic systems: (1) **MD17 dataset**: 8 small organic molecules (Aspirin,   
      Benzene, Ethanol, Malonaldehyde, Naphthalene, Salicylic acid, Toluene, Uracil). Pretraining uses 160K CMD configurations from 8 molecules. Fine-tuning 
      uses 1K training and 1K validation configurations. Tested on SchNet, DimeNet, SphereNet, GemNet-T with and without GPIP. (2) **ISO17 dataset**: C7O2H10
      isomers with 2M CMD configurations for pretraining. Two settings tested: small (20K) and large (400K) fine-tuning datasets. Evaluated generalization on
      unknown molecules/unknown conformations tasks. Data efficiency tested by varying training data from 20K to 400K. (3) **Liquid water with PBCs**: 64    
      water molecules with TIP3P force field for CMD pretraining (100K configurations). Tested force prediction accuracy plus MD simulation metrics:         
      stability (simulation steps before failure), radial distribution function (RDF), and diffusion coefficient. (4) **Electrolyte solutions with PBCs**:   
      New dataset constructed with [Na]+/[Li]+ cations, [PF6]-/[Tf2N]- anions, and DME/EC+DMC solvents at 1M and 4M concentrations. 160K CMD configurations  
      for pretraining, 8K DFT-calculated configurations for fine-tuning. Tested on SchNet, PaiNN, EGNN. Ablation studies conducted to evaluate contribution  
      of each SSL task.', 'result': 'GPIP consistently improves performance across all benchmarks: (1) **MD17**: Force MAE reductions of 37-62% across all   
      models. GemNet-T-G achieves 0.160 kcal/mol on Aspirin vs 0.227 baseline. (2) **ISO17**: GPIP-SchNet with 10K data achieves MAE 9.95, outperforming     
      non-pretrained SchNet with 100K data (MAE 10.55), demonstrating 10x data efficiency. With 100K training data, GPIP-SchNet achieves MAE 6.16 vs         
      non-pretrained 6.81 with 200K data. Computational cost: GPIP requires ~230 CPU core hours + 1.2 GPU hours vs 53,000 CPU core hours for 100K additional 
      ab initio labels (~230x cheaper). (3) **Liquid water**: GPIP improves force MAE (SchNet: 9.5→8.31, DimeNet: 3.5→2.73, GemNet-T: 5.0→2.91) and          
      simulation stability (DimeNet: 4→18 steps, GemNet-T: 6→25 steps). RDF errors also reduced. (4) **Electrolyte solutions**: Energy MAE improvements of   
      28-54% (SchNet: 0.713→0.516, PaiNN: 0.397→0.163, EGNN: 1.006→0.468). Force MAE improvements of 16-58%. Ablation studies confirm all three SSL tasks are
