      relevant applications, and also provides a large bibliography.', 'published': '2016-09-15', 'pdf_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/1609.04846v1',           
      'categories': ['cs.NE']}, {'arxiv_id': '2605.01304v1', 'title': 'SR-CGCNN: Shared Recurrent Convolution in Crystal Graph Neural Networks for Materials 
      Property Prediction', 'authors': ['Satadeep Bhattacharjee'], 'summary': 'Crystal graph neural networks predict materials properties by propagating     
      information through local atomic environments. In conventional crystal graph convolutional neural networks (CGCNNs), this propagation depth is         
      increased by stacking independently parameterized convolutional layers. This coupling between message-passing depth and parameter count raises a simple
      question: can repeated application of the same learned local update recover most of the benefit of a deeper CGCNN? We address this question by         
      introducing a shared-recurrent CGCNN (SR-CGCNN), in which the main crystal-graph convolutional weights are tied across recurrent message-passing steps.
      The graph construction, pooling operation, and prediction head are kept unchanged, allowing a controlled comparison with standard CGCNN baselines. On  
      Materials Project-derived formation-energy and band-gap datasets, a three-step SR-CGCNN approaches the accuracy of a standard three-layer CGCNN while  
      using only $34.5\\%$ of its trainable convolutional parameters. The formation-energy test mean absolute error changes from $0.0945$ to                 
