      and other benchmarks., keyword: DenseGNN, over-smoothing, dense connectivity, LOPE, deep GNN, content: {'background': "Modern generative models can    
      design millions of hypothetical materials, but screening requires fast and accurate property prediction models. GNNs have become the focus of research 
      due to their ability to directly act on graphical representations of molecules and materials. However, GNNs face several critical problems: (1) Nested 
      graph networks (ALIGNN, coNGN) that introduce bond angle information significantly increase trainable parameters and training costs, maintaining       
      advantages only on some crystal datasets. (2) Extending GNN models to broader domains (molecules, crystals, catalysis) and adapting to small datasets  
      remains challenging—Matbench data shows GNNs generally perform worse than MODNet on small datasets. (3) Most message-passing GNNs suffer from          
      over-smoothing, where node representation vectors become indistinguishable as GC layers increase, limiting model depth. These bottlenecks motivate     
      DenseGNN's design: efficiently embed many-body interactions without nested graphs, support very deep architectures without over-smoothing, and maintain
      strong performance across diverse domains and dataset sizes.", 'method': 'DenseGNN combines three key strategies: (1) Dense Connectivity Network (DCN):
      Unlike traditional sequential connections (L connections for L layers), DCN connects all blocks to each other, introducing L(L+1)/2 connections. This  
      enables direct information flow from all preceding layers, implicit deep supervision via direct gradient access, and regularization effect reducing    
      overfitting on small datasets. The architecture supports very deep GNNs while avoiding over-smoothing. (2) Hierarchical Node-Edge Graph Residual       
      Networks (HRN): Simultaneously updates edge, node, and graph-level features during message passing through residual connections. Edge update:          
      e_ij^(t+1) = e_ij^t + Σ σ([v_i^t ⊕ v_j^t ⊕ u_ij^t ⊕ e_ij^t]W_1^t + b_1^t). Node update: v_i^(t+1) = v_i^t + Σ σ([v_i^t ⊕ u_ij^t ⊕ e_ij^t]W_2^t +       
      b_2^t). Graph update: u_ij^(t+1) = u_ij^t + Σ σ([v_i^t ⊕ u_ij^t ⊕ e_ij^t]W_3^t + b_3^t). (3) Local Structure Order Parameters Embedding (LOPE):        
      Reflects local environment and coordination of atoms through atomic embeddings and orientation-resolved embeddings, obtained by calculating the product
      integral of radial distribution function (RDF) and Gaussian window function. This captures bond angle information without nested graph networks,       
      substantially reducing training costs. The architecture uses KNN edge selection (k=12 or 6), 32 Gaussian functions for edge distance expansion in      
      [0,8]Å range (E(3)-invariant), 128-dimensional feature space, swish activation, and Adam optimizer with linear learning rate scheduler for 300 epochs. 
      DenseGNN-Lite variant reduces parameters while maintaining DCN and LOPE strategies.', 'experiment': 'Comprehensive evaluation across multiple domains: 
      (1) Matbench (8 regression tasks): DenseGNN outperforms ALIGNN, SchNet, M3GNet, MODNet, coGN, coNGN on most tasks including formation energy (0.0179   
      eV/atom vs 0.0170 coGN), band gap (0.1551 eV vs 0.1559 coGN), phonons (24.85 1/cm vs 28.89 coNGN), with notable advantages on small datasets (Jdft2d:  
      32.54 vs 37.17 coGN, 8.95% improvement). (2) QM9 (130,829 molecules): DenseGNN achieves competitive results on HOMO (0.0261 eV), LUMO (0.0216 eV),     
      energy gap (0.0423 eV), approaching DimeNet++ performance. DenseNGN (nested variant with DCN) surpasses DimeNet++ on most tasks, validating DCN        
      strategy importance. (3) JARVIS-DFT: DenseGNN outperforms coGN, CGCNN, Matminer, CFID on most property prediction tasks. (4) Molecular datasets (Lipop,
      FreeSolv, ESOL): Competitive performance across different property prediction tasks. (5) OC22 catalysis: DenseNGN shows competitive performance on     
      IS2RES task. Training parameters: DenseGNN (1.72M), DenseGNN-Lite (771K), DenseNGN (3.10M), compared to ALIGNN (7.36M), coNGN (2.80M). All models      
      implemented on KGCNN framework with unified JSON configuration, 300 epochs training.', 'result': 'DenseGNN achieves SOTA performance across crystal,   
      molecular, and catalysis domains while addressing key GNN limitations. Main findings: (1) DCN enables very deep GNNs (T=5 blocks) without              
      over-smoothing degradation, with dense connections providing implicit deep supervision and regularization. (2) LOPE strategy efficiently captures      
      many-body interactions (bond angles, local geometric distortions) without nested graph networks, reducing training costs while maintaining accuracy.   
      DenseGNN requires fewer edges than MEGNet, SchNet, CGCNN, coGN even with dense connections. (3) Strong performance on small datasets (Jdft2d, Phonons, 
      Dielectric) addresses the challenge where GNNs typically underperform. (4) Universal applicability: same architecture works across crystals (Matbench, 
      JARVIS-DFT), molecules (QM9, Lipop, FreeSolv, ESOL), and catalysis (OC22). (5) DenseGNN-Lite (771K parameters) maintains competitive performance with  
      significantly reduced parameters. (6) Structural embeddings from pre-trained models approach XRD method in distinguishing crystal structures.          
      Limitations: Dense connections may slow inference speed; LOPE requires careful atomic embedding optimization. The work demonstrates that architectural 
