      leading to inconsistent featurizations under trivial translations along the backbone. These issues cause unreliable property predictions, particularly 
      problematic for discovery workflows where large errors could lead to thermodynamically unstable polymer designs. The motivation is to develop a        
      representation specifically optimized for polymer structures rather than adapting molecular representations.', 'method': 'The core innovation is the   
      polymer graph representation that explicitly encodes periodicity. Starting from a trimer graph (three repeat units), a periodic edge is added between  
      the two terminal atoms, creating a closed ring structure. This design ensures: (1) unique representations for distinct polymers, (2) correct bonding   
      environments for all atoms including terminals, and (3) natural periodicity that enables message passing throughout the extended chain. The periodic   
      edge allows messages to flow directly between atoms that would be adjacent in the infinite polymer chain. The graph uses learned feature vectors for   
      atoms (nodes) and bonds (edges), updated through message passing. The MPNN architecture consists of 3 message passing layers with 64 hidden dimensions.
      Messages are aggregated from neighboring atoms and bonds, with the periodic edge treated identically to regular covalent bonds. After message passing, 
      atom features are aggregated via sum pooling and passed through a 2-layer feedforward network for property prediction. The learned descriptors in the  
      final hidden layer are optimized for specific properties during training, eliminating the need for hand-crafted features.', 'experiment': 'Dataset:    
      15,219 data points across 10 polymer properties spanning thermal (glass transition temperature Tg, crystallization tendency), structural (density),    
      optical (refractive index, dielectric constant), and electronic (atomization energy, band gap chain/bulk, electron affinity, ionization energy)        
      properties. Properties from experimental (PolyInfo: Tg, density) and computational (DFT: others) sources, covering 9,935 unique homopolymers. Training 
      protocol: 5-fold cross-validation with RMSE as metric. Models compared: (1) RF/Molecular Descriptors (200 RDKit features from monomer SMILES), (2)     
      MPNN/Monomer Graph, (3) MPNN/Dimer Graph, (4) MPNN/Trimer Graph, (5) MPNN/Polymer Graph. Ablation studies systematically removed periodic edge and     
      varied chain length. For learned descriptor analysis, MPNN-learned encodings were extracted and used as input to RF models to isolate representation   
      quality from model architecture effects. Correlation analysis between learned features and molecular descriptors provided interpretability.', 'result':
      'Main findings: (1) Polymer graph achieves mean normalized RMSE of 0.800 vs 1.000 for RF/Molecular Descriptors and 0.875 for MPNN/Monomer Graph - a 20%
